window.GEO_DATA={"countries":[{"n":"Afghanistan","dom":".af","side":"R","call":"+93","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate, black text, province name in Dari/Pashto; very limited coverage.","pos":"Afghanistan: wooden or concrete poles with loose, sagging wires. Coverage is very limited (mostly Kabul).","arch":"Afghanistan: flat-roofed mud-brick houses in earth tones, stacked on hillsides behind high compound walls.","ps":"bg=white; top=کابل KABUL; txt=۱۲۳۴۵ 12345","lang":"Dari, Pashto","script":"سړک · جاده · ړ ښ ځ ټ","note":"Persian-Arabic script with the Pashto letters ړ ښ ځ ټ ډ, which Arabic and Iranian Persian don't use. Dari and Pashto often share one board.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; cables=yes","pot":"Afghanistan: thin wooden or concrete poles with loose, sagging wires.","ro":"warn=tri-white; word=سړک","ros":"Afghanistan: Dari and Pashto signs, sometimes with English; roads often unmarked, dusty and lined with compound walls."},{"n":"Åland Islands","dom":".ax","side":"R","call":"+358","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate, blue left band with Åland flag and ÅL; Finnish style.","ps":"band=blue; code=ÅL","lang":"Swedish","script":"gatan · vägen · å ä ö","note":"Swedish, not Finnish: Åland is Swedish-speaking, so look for å and -vägen / -gatan. Finnish would show double vowels (aa, ää) and -tie / -katu.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-yellow; word=gatan / vägen","ros":"Åland: Swedish-only street names (-vägen, -gatan), unlike bilingual mainland Finland."},{"n":"Albania","dom":".al","side":"R","call":"+355","plate":"DOUBLE BLUE: Long white plate with blue strips on left (AL) and right (like Italy).","pos":"Albania: concrete poles with messy bundled wires, often beside unfinished buildings.","arch":"Albania: unfinished concrete-frame houses with exposed rebar and red tile roofs are everywhere.","ps":"band=eu-double; code=AL","lang":"Albanian","script":"Rruga · ë ç · sh xh gj","note":"Albanian 'Rruga' (Rr.) with ë and ç. Kosovo also writes Albanian, but often adds Serbian 'Ulica'; Albania rarely does.","ro":"word=Rruga","ros":"Albania: streets are 'Rruga' (Rr.); the letters ë and ç give Albanian away.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; cap=black-third; refl=red-rect","bot":"Albania: white post with the top third painted black and a red rectangle on the right side (white on the left)."},{"n":"American Samoa","dom":".as","side":"R","call":"+1 684","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy US-size plate reading 'American Samoa'.","arch":"American Samoa: open-sided fale and simple concrete houses with tin roofs in dense tropical green.","ps":"size=na; top=AMERICAN SAMOA; txt=1234","lang":"Samoan, English","script":"Ala · Road · ā ō","note":"Samoan words (fale, ala = road) with macrons, on US-style yellow signs.","ro":"warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"American Samoa: US-style signs in a Polynesian setting; right-hand traffic."},{"n":"Andorra","dom":".ad","side":"R","call":"+376","plate":"WHITE + CREST: Compact white plate with colorful coat of arms on left and 'AND'.","arch":"Andorra: grey stone houses with dark slate roofs packed into narrow Pyrenean valleys.","ps":"band=none; crest=yes; size=short","lang":"Catalan","script":"Carrer · Avinguda · l·l ç à","note":"Catalan, not Spanish: 'Carrer' (not Calle) and 'Avinguda' (not Avenida). The dotted 'l·l' is unique to Catalan.","ro":"word=Carrer","ros":"Andorra: Catalan names ('Carrer', 'Avinguda'); look for l·l and à / è / ò."},{"n":"Antarctica","dom":".aq","side":"R","call":"+672","plate":"NONE: No regular civilian plates."},{"n":"Argentina","dom":".ar","side":"R","call":"+54","plate":"MERCOSUR / BLACK: Long white with blue top band, or older black plate with white center.","pos":"Argentina: round concrete poles with three wires in an alternating pattern; doubled A-shaped poles; wooden poles in the Pampas.","arch":"Argentina: low single-storey brick or plastered houses with flat roofs in wide open land.","ps":"topbar=blue; top=REPÚBLICA ARGENTINA; txt=AB 123 CD","lang":"Spanish (Rioplatense)","script":"Calle · Avenida · Ruta Nacional (RN) · PARE","note":"Shares Mercosur plates and Rioplatense Spanish (vos) with Uruguay. Argentina: red-on-white chevrons, no bollards, YPF petrol stations. Uruguay: white bollards with a yellow side and triple centre lines.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"Argentina: round concrete pole carrying three wires in an alternating pattern; doubled, A-shaped poles are common.","ro":"center=yellow-white; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Avenida","ros":"Argentina: white dashed centre beside a solid yellow line (or double yellow); red-on-white chevrons, unique in South America; pentagon RN shields, square RP shields; small black-and-white kilometre posts; YPF petrol stations.","bos":"Argentina: no bollards; small white kilometre posts with a black top showing the km and RN or RP."},{"n":"Armenia","dom":".am","side":"R","call":"+374","plate":"FLAG BAND: Long white plate, red-blue-orange flag and AM on the left.","pos":"Armenia: Soviet-style concrete poles and yellow overground gas pipes along village roads.","arch":"Armenia: buildings of pink or orange tuff stone are a strong Armenia clue.","ps":"band=flag; flag=red-blue-orange; code=AM; txt=12 AB 345","lang":"Armenian","script":"փողոց · Ա Բ Գ · ու","note":"Armenian has its own alphabet with hooked letters (ա բ գ դ) and many 'ու'. Georgian looks rounder and has no capital letters.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; top=crossarm","pot":"Armenia: grey Soviet-era concrete poles; yellow gas pipes often run beside the road.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=փողոց","ros":"Armenia: unique Armenian script with rounded, hooked letters (Ա Բ Գ)."},{"n":"Aruba","dom":".aw","side":"R","call":"+297","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate reading 'ARUBA'.","arch":"Aruba: brightly painted Dutch-Caribbean cottages among cactus and dry scrub.","ps":"size=na; top=ARUBA; txt=A-12345; sub=ONE HAPPY ISLAND","lang":"Papiamento, Dutch","script":"Caya · Straat · Weg","note":"Papiamento is shared with Curaçao, but Aruba writes Caya with a C (Curaçao: Kaya). Aruba is flat and arid with divi-divi trees and cactus; plates read ARUBA.","ro":"word=Caya","ros":"Aruba: Dutch-style signs; Papiamento street names ('Caya'); dry, flat land with cactus and divi-divi trees."},{"n":"Australia","dom":".au","side":"L","call":"+61","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Medium slim plates; white in most states, yellow in New South Wales.","pos":"Australia: Stobie poles (steel and concrete) in South Australia; tall wooden poles with long crossarms elsewhere.","arch":"Australia: single-storey brick or weatherboard houses with corrugated iron or tile roofs and wide verandas.","ps":"bg=white; txt=1AB 2CD","lang":"English","script":"Rd · St · Cct · Hwy","note":"Australian English signs; New Zealand adds Māori names (Wh-, ā) and uses 'SH' state highways.","po":"mat=steel; form=stobie; top=crossarm","pot":"Australia: 'Stobie pole' in South Australia, two steel I-beams filled with concrete.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"Australia: white dashed outer edge lines; signs use Rd / St / Hwy / Cct.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; refl=red-rect; thin=yes","bot":"Australia: thin, flat, flexible white post with a red rectangle on the left and white on the right."},{"n":"Austria","dom":".at","side":"R","call":"+43","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (A), thin red top/bottom borders.","pos":"Austria: wooden or concrete poles, often A-frame pairs, with tidy Alpine wiring.","arch":"Austria: Alpine chalets with a white plastered ground floor, dark timber upper floor and flower boxes.","ps":"band=eu; code=A; border=red-tb","lang":"German","script":"Straße · Gasse · ä ö ü ß","note":"Same German as Germany, but 'Gasse' for small streets is much more common. Switzerland and Liechtenstein write 'Strasse' without ß.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Straße / Gasse","ros":"Austria: white lines; street names end in -straße or -gasse and keep the ß (Switzerland drops it).","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=red-rect","bot":"Austria: white post with a slanted black top and a red front reflector (white on the back).","bos":"Austria: white post with a slanted black top and a red rectangular reflector on the front."},{"n":"Bangladesh","dom":".bd","side":"L","call":"+880","plate":"WHITE / GREEN: White (private) or green (commercial) plate with Bengali script.","pos":"Bangladesh: concrete poles overloaded with cables in busy towns on flat delta land.","arch":"Bangladesh: corrugated tin-roofed village houses and mud walls; dense brick in towns.","ps":"bg=white; size=short; top=ঢাকা মেট্রো-গ; txt=১২-৩৪৫৬","lang":"Bengali (Bangla)","script":"সড়ক · রোড · ক খ গ","note":"Bengali script hangs from a continuous top line (ক খ গ). India's West Bengal also writes Bengali, but Bangladesh plates are in Bengali digits (ঢাকা মেট্রো) and India's are Latin.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; cables=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Bangladesh: slim concrete poles overloaded with cable bundles.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=সড়ক","ros":"Bangladesh: left-hand traffic; green CNG auto-rickshaws and cycle rickshaws everywhere; Bengali signs with English on highways.","bos":"Bangladesh: no standard roadside posts; kerbs and tree trunks are often painted black and white or yellow and black."},{"n":"Belarus","dom":".by","side":"R","call":"+375","plate":"FLAG BAND: Long white plate, red-green flag and BY on the left.","pos":"Belarus: concrete poles, often with a diagonal concrete support strut (as in Russia).","arch":"Belarus: painted wooden village houses with decorative window frames and picket fences.","ps":"band=flag; flag=red-green; code=BY","lang":"Belarusian / Russian","script":"вуліца · і ў","note":"Belarusian uses і and ў (вуліца); Russian uses и and ы (улица); Ukrainian has і ї є but never ў (вулиця).","po":"mat=concrete; form=octagonal; strut=yes; base=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Belarus: octagonal grey concrete pole held up by a diagonal concrete strut, with a white-painted base.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=вуліца","ros":"Belarus: Cyrillic with і and ў (unique to Belarusian); 'вуліца' = street.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; band=black-diag; refl=red-rect","bot":"Belarus: white post with a diagonal black stripe across the upper half and a small red-white reflector inside it."},{"n":"Belgium","dom":".be","side":"R","call":"+32","plate":"WHITE + RED TEXT: Long white plate with blue EU strip (B) and dark red characters.","pos":"Belgium: concrete poles in villages, often carrying a streetlight arm.","arch":"Belgium: mismatched detached brick houses of different styles standing side by side.","ps":"band=eu; code=B; ink=red","lang":"Dutch / French / German","script":"straat · Rue · laan","note":"Flanders writes Dutch 'straat' like the Netherlands, Wallonia writes French 'Rue' like France; Brussels signs show both.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=straat / Rue","ros":"Belgium: Dutch 'Straat' in Flanders, French 'Rue' in Wallonia, both in Brussels."},{"n":"Belize","dom":".bz","side":"R","call":"+501","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy white plate with district name.","pos":"Belize: wooden poles among tropical vegetation, Caribbean coastal style.","arch":"Belize: painted wooden houses raised on stilts with tin roofs.","ps":"size=na; top=BELIZE; txt=C 12345","lang":"English, Kriol, Spanish","script":"Street · Road · Highway · STOP","note":"The only English-speaking country in Central America, next to Spanish-speaking Mexico and Guatemala. Unlike most English-speaking Caribbean islands, Belize drives on the right.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Street","ros":"Belize: English signs with a Caribbean feel; US-size plates."},{"n":"Bermuda","dom":".bm","side":"L","call":"+1 441","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate reading 'BERMUDA'.","pos":"Bermuda: ornate wooden poles, larger in towns.","arch":"Bermuda: pastel blue, pink and yellow boxy houses with white stepped limestone roofs that catch rainwater.","ps":"size=na; top=BERMUDA; txt=12345","lang":"English","script":"Road · Lane · STOP","note":"Keeps left like the UK and feels British (street signs on building walls, stone walls as guardrails), but sits in the Atlantic: pastel houses with white stepped limestone roofs are unique to Bermuda.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid","ros":"Bermuda: keeps left; a single yellow centre line, often faded; narrow lanes with stone walls or wooden fences as guardrails; street signs fixed to building walls."},{"n":"Bhutan","dom":".bt","side":"L","call":"+975","plate":"RED / YELLOW: Red plate (private) or yellow (commercial) with white/black text.","arch":"Bhutan: whitewashed houses with ornate painted wooden windows and wide overhanging roofs.","ps":"bg=red; ink=white; txt=BP-1-A1234","lang":"Dzongkha, English","script":"ལམ · Lam · Dzong","note":"Dzongkha uses the Tibetan script: letters hang from a line and stack vertically, with dots (་) between syllables. Nepal's Devanagari has a straight top bar instead.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Lam","ros":"Bhutan: winding mountain roads with painted parapet stones; signs in Dzongkha and English; 'Lam' = road; red private plates."},{"n":"Bolivia","dom":".bo","side":"R","call":"+591","plate":"BLUE TOP: Short boxy white plate with blue top band (red for public transit).","pos":"Bolivia: concrete poles with sparse wiring on the dry, high Altiplano.","arch":"Bolivia: adobe houses with thatch or tin roofs in the highlands.","ps":"size=na; ink=blue; top=BOLIVIA; txt=1234 ABC","lang":"Spanish, Quechua, Aymara, Guaraní","script":"Calle · Avenida · PARE","note":"Plates are pale with blue characters (1234 ABC). The high, treeless Altiplano and thick wooden sign posts are typical; Peru has mototaxis and Jirón, Bolivia has neither.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Bolivia: yellow centre, white edges, but many paved roads have no lines; signs on thick unpainted wooden posts; stop signs say PARE; inconsistent sign colours.","bos":"Bolivia: several bollard styles, some without a white reflector; corner posts with coloured reflectors act like chevrons."},{"n":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","dom":".ba","side":"R","call":"+387","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (BIH) and no regional codes.","arch":"Bosnia and Herzegovina: Ottoman-era houses in Mostar and Sarajevo; elsewhere unfinished brick houses with red roofs.","ps":"band=blue; code=BIH","lang":"Bosnian / Serbian / Croatian","script":"ulica · улица · č ć š ž đ","note":"Latin and Cyrillic both appear, Cyrillic mostly in Republika Srpska. Croatia is Latin-only; Serbia mixes both.","ro":"word=ulica","ros":"Bosnia and Herzegovina: street signs mix Latin 'Ulica' and Cyrillic 'Улица'."},{"n":"Botswana","dom":".bw","side":"L","call":"+267","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear, black text: B, three digits, three letters (B 123 ABC).","arch":"Botswana: round thatched huts in villages; low modern houses in towns.","ps":"rear=yellow; txt=B 123 ABC","lang":"English, Setswana","script":"Road · Street · A1 · Gabo- · Ma- · Se-","note":"Setswana names (Mo-, Ma-, Se-, Tl-, -ng: Mogoditshane, Tlokweng). Green signs with the route letter A (A1, A2, A3) are Botswana's; South Africa uses N and R. The land is flatter and emptier than South Africa.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Botswana: white centre (sometimes triple), yellow edges; green signs with yellow A route numbers; sign posts and kerbs painted black and yellow in and near towns; coverage mostly along the A1, A2 and A3.","bos":"Botswana: bollards are rare: a triangular post with the kilometre on its side near the cities, and reflector pairs (red and white arrow) at drainage ditches."},{"n":"Brazil","dom":".br","side":"R","call":"+55","plate":"MERCOSUR / GREY: Long white plate with blue top band (Mercosur) or older grey plate.","pos":"Brazil: concrete poles topped with a Y- or trident-shaped crossarm.","arch":"Brazil: colourful colonial facades in old towns; brick-and-concrete houses elsewhere.","ps":"topbar=blue; top=BRASIL; txt=ABC1D23","lang":"Portuguese","script":"Rua · Avenida · Rodovia · PARE · ção","note":"The only Portuguese-speaking country in the Americas: ão, ç, nh / lh and words ending in -ção. Spanish-speaking neighbours write Calle and ñ.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=slots; top=trident","pot":"Brazil: concrete ladder pole with cutouts and a Y- or trident-shaped crossarm on top.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Rua / Avenida","ros":"Brazil: yellow centre (often double), white edges; black sign backs; yellow-on-black chevrons; blue Rua / Avenida street signs; BR-xxx national roads and state roads (SP-, PR-, MG-…) with km plates; phone area codes (DDD) on shop signs.","bos":"Brazil: no bollards; yellow-on-black chevrons and kilometre plates (BR or state code, road number, km)."},{"n":"British Indian Ocean Territory","dom":".io","side":"R","call":"+246","plate":"NONE: No regular civilian plates.","arch":"British Indian Ocean Territory: military base buildings on Diego Garcia; no civilian settlement.","ps":"bg=white; top=DIEGO GARCIA; txt=1234","lang":"English","script":"Road · Diego Garcia","note":"Diego Garcia is a military base: English signs and US-style infrastructure on a flat coral atoll.","ros":"British Indian Ocean Territory: flat coral atoll roads with US military signage."},{"n":"Bulgaria","dom":".bg","side":"R","call":"+359","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (BG) and Cyrillic-shared letters.","pos":"Bulgaria: concrete poles, frequently with stork nests in villages.","arch":"Bulgaria: houses with a stone ground floor, timber upper floor and red tile roofs.","ps":"band=eu; code=BG","lang":"Bulgarian","script":"улица (ул.) · ъ щ","note":"Bulgarian Cyrillic uses ъ as a vowel and щ a lot; it has no ы or э (Russian) and no ђ ћ (Serbian).","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=улица (ул.)","ros":"Bulgaria: Cyrillic with ъ and щ; 'ул.' = street (no Serbian љ / њ)."},{"n":"Cambodia","dom":".kh","side":"R","call":"+855","plate":"BLUE TOP: North American size; white plate with blue top stripe.","pos":"Cambodia: concrete poles with huge tangled bundles of black cable.","arch":"Cambodia: wooden houses on tall stilts with steep roofs, often over water.","ps":"size=na; topbar=blue; top=ភ្នំពេញ PHNOM PENH; txt=2AB-1234","lang":"Khmer","script":"ផ្លូវ · ក ខ គ · Phnom","note":"Khmer is ornate, with tall 'crowns' on letters and stacked subscripts below the line. Thai has small loops and fewer stacks; Lao is rounder and simpler.","po":"mat=concrete; form=square; holes=square-holes; cables=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Cambodia: square concrete pole with small holes most of the way up and heavy tangled black cables.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=ផ្លូវ","ros":"Cambodia: red dirt roadsides; Khmer signs with English; motos pulling remork trailers; national roads numbered NR."},{"n":"Canada","dom":".ca","side":"R","call":"+1","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate; diverse province designs (many omit front plates).","pos":"Canada: wooden poles with pole-mounted transformers, very similar to the US.","arch":"Canada: clapboard and vinyl-sided houses; steep metal roofs in Quebec.","ps":"size=na; top=ONTARIO; txt=ABCD 123; sub=YOURS TO DISCOVER","lang":"English, French","script":"Street · Rue · ARRÊT · MAXIMUM","note":"Quebec is French-only (Rue, Chemin, ARRÊT) but looks North American: yellow centre lines, US-style poles. New Brunswick signs are bilingual (West / Ouest). Speed signs say MAXIMUM in km/h, unlike the US's SPEED LIMIT in mph.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm; transformer=yes","pot":"Canada: tall wooden pole with a horizontal crossarm and a grey cylindrical transformer.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Rue / Street","ros":"Canada: yellow centre lines (a single line more often than in the US), white edges; MAXIMUM speed signs in km/h; black-and-yellow checkerboard dead-end signs; route shields and kilometre posts differ by province; Quebec stop signs say ARRÊT.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; refl=red-rect; thin=yes","bot":"Quebec: long, thin white posts fixed to the guardrails, with a red or green reflector on top.","bos":"Canada: no national bollard. Quebec has thin white guardrail posts with a red or green reflector on top; New Brunswick has flat yellow posts on the Trans-Canada Highway."},{"n":"Chile","dom":".cl","side":"R","call":"+56","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate with black text and small central crest (no blue band).","pos":"Chile: square concrete poles with indents on both sides and pinholes near the top; wooden poles in the south.","arch":"Chile: wooden shingle houses in the south (Chiloé); adobe in the north.","ps":"bg=white; top=CHILE; txt=BB·BB·12","lang":"Spanish (Mapudungun place names)","script":"Calle · Pasaje · Ruta · PARE","note":"White centre lines and long white plates with no blue band separate Chile from Argentina (Mercosur plates, a yellow line beside white dashes). Mapudungun names end in -hue, -co, -che.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=slots; top=crossarm","pot":"Chile: square concrete pole with indents on both sides and small pinholes near the top (fewer cross-pieces than Brazil).","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Pasaje","ros":"Chile: white centre lines, unique in South America (yellow in the far south, all-yellow in Tierra del Fuego); blue direction signs; thin sign posts with a slot at the top; Ruta 5 runs the length of the country; local road letters run A to Y from north to south.","bos":"Chile: bollards are uncommon; where present they resemble Spain's (white with a dark top)."},{"n":"China","dom":".cn","side":"R","call":"+86","plate":"BLUE: Blue plate with white text and province character first; green plates for EVs. Landmark-only coverage.","pos":"China: concrete poles with dense wiring. Landmark-only coverage in GeoGuessr.","arch":"China: brick or stone houses with curved tile roofs; tall apartment blocks in cities.","ps":"bg=blue; ink=white; txt=京A·12345","lang":"Chinese (Simplified)","script":"路 · 街 · 东 · 简体","note":"Simplified characters (东, 门, 广) set mainland China apart from Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau, which write Traditional (東, 門, 廣).","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-yellow; word=路","ros":"China: Simplified Chinese characters; blue / green road signs with pinyin."},{"n":"Christmas Island","dom":".cx","side":"L","call":"+61","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Australian-style slim plate.","arch":"Christmas Island: tropical houses and Malay-Chinese community buildings surrounded by rainforest.","ps":"bg=white; top=CHRISTMAS ISLAND; txt=1234","lang":"English (Malay, Chinese)","script":"Road · Jalan · 路","note":"Australian territory: Australian-style signs, with Malay and Chinese names in the Kampong and Poon Saan.","ros":"Christmas Island: Australian-style signs and left-hand traffic in thick rainforest; red crab crossing signs."},{"n":"Cocos (Keeling) Islands","dom":".cc","side":"L","call":"+61","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Australian-style slim plate.","arch":"Cocos (Keeling) Islands: low houses among coconut palms on flat coral islands.","ps":"bg=white; top=COCOS ISLANDS; txt=123","lang":"English, Cocos Malay","script":"Road · Jalan","note":"Australian territory; Home Island speaks Cocos Malay."},{"n":"Colombia","dom":".co","side":"R","call":"+57","plate":"YELLOW: Short boxy bright yellow plates front and rear (white for commercial).","pos":"Colombia: concrete poles with a small X-shaped brace under the top crossbar (the 'Colombian cross').","arch":"Colombia: colourful colonial houses with balconies; brick and tile roofs in the Andes.","ps":"bg=yellow; size=na; txt=ABC 123; sub=BOGOTÁ D.C.","lang":"Spanish","script":"Calle · Carrera (Cra.) · PARE","note":"Calle plus Carrera (Cra / Kr) numbered addresses are uniquely Colombian. Plates are yellow front and rear (Ecuador: white with the province letter first), and Colombian road signs have a grey cross on the back.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; top=xbrace","pot":"Colombia: a small diagonal metal cross-brace supports the top crossarm (the 'Colombian cross').","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Carrera","ros":"Colombia: yellow centre, white edges; a grey cross on the back of road signs; slim white sign posts; stop signs say PARE; tall green kilometre markers topped with 'PR'; Calle and Carrera (Cra.) grid.","bos":"Colombia: bollards are uncommon; tall green kilometre markers topped with 'PR', and older stone markers with black lettering."},{"n":"Costa Rica","dom":".cr","side":"R","call":"+506","plate":"BLUE TOP: Short boxy white plate with blue top banner and black numbers.","pos":"Costa Rica: wooden and concrete poles along lush, green tropical roadsides.","arch":"Costa Rica: single-storey concrete houses with tin roofs and metal window grilles.","ps":"size=na; topbar=blue; top=COSTA RICA; txt=ABC-123","lang":"Spanish","script":"Calle · Avenida · ALTO · Pulpería","note":"Stop signs say ALTO (Panama next door writes PARE). Most coverage is Trekker (beaches, San José, Caño and Cocos islands), so look for the walker's shadow. Addresses are given as distances from landmarks ('100 m norte').","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Costa Rica: mostly Trekker coverage; where roads are marked, a single yellow centre line; stop signs say ALTO; red taxis with a yellow triangle on the door; addresses by landmark ('100 m norte')."},{"n":"Croatia","dom":".hr","side":"R","call":"+385","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (HR) and red-white checkered shield.","pos":"Croatia: wooden and concrete poles; bare limestone landscapes on the coast.","arch":"Croatia: brick houses inland (Slavonia); white stone houses with orange roofs on the coast.","ps":"band=eu; code=HR; crest=yes","lang":"Croatian","script":"ulica · č ć š ž đ","note":"Latin only. Same 'ulica' as Slovak and Serbian Latin, but đ and ć are not Slovak; Polish 'ulica' would show ł ą ę.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=ulica","ros":"Croatia: 'Ulica' with č ć đ š ž; white road lines."},{"n":"Curaçao","dom":".cw","side":"R","call":"+599","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate.","pos":"Curaçao: many poles have about 2 m of white paint at the base; red-and-white striped poles at street corners.","arch":"Curaçao: pastel Dutch colonial buildings with gabled facades (Willemstad).","lang":"Papiamentu, Dutch, English","script":"Kaya · Weg · Straat","note":"Papiamentu writes Kaya with a K (Aruba: Caya). All-white road lines and poles with a white-painted base; colourful Dutch colonial façades in Willemstad.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; base=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Curaçao: pole with about 2 m of white paint at the base.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=Kaya","ros":"Curaçao: all-white road lines; blue destination signs on small roads; black-and-white striped traffic-light poles; tan guardrails; 'Kaya' street names."},{"n":"Cyprus","dom":".cy","side":"L","call":"+357","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate (or older yellow rear) with blue EU strip (CY).","arch":"Cyprus: plastered stone houses with flat roofs and rooftop solar water heaters.","ps":"band=eu; code=CY","lang":"Greek / Turkish","script":"Οδός · Odos","note":"Greek alphabet with a Latin transliteration under it; the Turkish-administered north writes 'Sokak'.","ros":"Cyprus: bilingual Greek-English street signs; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Czechia (Czech Republic)","dom":".cz","side":"R","call":"+420","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (CZ) and central round stickers.","pos":"Czechia: concrete poles beside tidy villages and fields.","arch":"Czechia: village cottages with steep roofs, gable end facing the street.","ps":"band=eu; code=CZ","lang":"Czech","script":"ulice · ř ě ů","note":"Czech 'ulice' vs Slovak, Polish and Croatian 'ulica'. ř, ě and ů are Czech-only; Slovak uses ä ľ ô instead.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=ulice","ros":"Czechia: Czech ř and ů; signs say 'ulice' or 'náměstí'.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=orange-2","bot":"Czechia: white post with a slanted black top and two horizontal orange reflectors stacked on the front.","bos":"Czechia: white post with a black top band and small orange reflectors on the front."},{"n":"Denmark","dom":".dk","side":"R","call":"+45","plate":"WHITE + RED: Long white plate with red border; yellow plates for commercial vehicles.","pos":"Denmark: overhead poles are rare because most cables are underground.","arch":"Denmark: red or yellow brick houses with tiled roofs; whitewashed farms.","ps":"band=none; border=red","lang":"Danish","script":"vej · gade · æ ø å","note":"Danish and Norwegian share æ ø; Swedish uses ä ö. Danish 'vej', Norwegian 'vei', Swedish 'väg'.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=vej / gade","ros":"Denmark: street names end in -vej or -gade; dashed outer edge lines.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=yellow-rect","bot":"Denmark: white post with a slanted black top and a yellow reflector."},{"n":"Dominican Republic","dom":".do","side":"R","call":"+1","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy white plate with top colored stripe (often rear-only).","pos":"Dominican Republic: square concrete poles, yellow-painted bases in Santo Domingo and grey in Santiago; octagonal poles in the suburbs.","arch":"Dominican Republic: brightly painted single-storey concrete houses with tin roofs.","ps":"size=na; top=REPÚBLICA DOMINICANA; txt=A123456","lang":"Spanish","script":"Calle · Colmado · Ens. · PARE","note":"Spanish with a roof-rack car: the Dominican rack has black rubber caps in the middle (Guatemala's is bare). Stop signs say PARE, not ALTO. Coverage is mostly Santo Domingo and Santiago.","po":"mat=concrete; form=square; base=yellow; top=crossarm","pot":"Dominican Republic: square concrete pole; a yellow-painted base points to Santo Domingo, grey to Santiago.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Dominican Republic: yellow centre lines, white edges; stop signs say PARE; green street signs with a white border; concrete highway dividers with a red top reflector; kilometre markers reading 'Rep. Dom.'.","bos":"Dominican Republic: no roadside bollards; concrete highway dividers carry a red reflector on top and yellow ones on the sides."},{"n":"Ecuador","dom":".ec","side":"R","call":"+593","plate":"WHITE / ORANGE: Short boxy white plate (private) or orange top band (commercial).","pos":"Ecuador: round concrete poles and ladder-style poles with small step indents; bases sometimes painted.","arch":"Ecuador: colonial houses with wooden balconies in Quito; adobe in the highlands.","ps":"size=na; top=ECUADOR; txt=PBC-1234","lang":"Spanish, Kichwa","script":"Calle · Avenida · Vía · PARE","note":"The first plate letter gives the province (P Pichincha, G Guayas, A Azuay); commercial plates have an orange top or are fully orange. Round bollards with red rings are common here and rare elsewhere in Latin America.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=steps; top=crossarm","pot":"Ecuador: concrete pole with small step indents, shorter than Brazil's; bases are sometimes coloured.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Ecuador: yellow centre line, white edges; concrete roads and double guardrails; stop signs say PARE; blue shields for primary E-roads (E10–E50: ending 0 = east-west, 5 = north-south); plain sign backs.","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=red-2ring","bot":"Ecuador: small round white bollard with two red rings.","bos":"Ecuador: bollards are common, most often a small round white post with two red rings; also flat black posts with two red reflectors."},{"n":"Egypt","dom":".eg","side":"R","call":"+20","plate":"LIGHT BLUE TOP: Long plate with light-blue top band reading EGYPT, Arabic letters and digits. Landmark-only coverage.","arch":"Egypt: sand-coloured stone and brick, minarets and domes; the pyramids at Giza.","ps":"topbar=blue; top=EGYPT مصر; txt=٤٥٦٧ ب ج د","lang":"Arabic (English at sights)","script":"شارع · ٠١٢٣٤٥ · Street","note":"Trekker only, at four spots: the Giza pyramids, Mari Gerges street in Coptic Cairo, the Muhammad Ali mosque and Qaitbay citadel in Alexandria. Plates and prices use Eastern Arabic digits (٠–٩).","ro":"word=شارع","ros":"Egypt: walking-only landmark coverage; Arabic and English signs at the sights; phone codes 02 Cairo, 03 Alexandria."},{"n":"Estonia","dom":".ee","side":"R","call":"+372","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (EST) and 3 digits + 3 letters.","pos":"Estonia: wooden poles, often with a supporting strut, among forests.","arch":"Estonia: wooden farmhouses with steep roofs; Soviet-era blocks in towns.","ps":"band=eu; code=EST","lang":"Estonian","script":"tänav · õ ä ö ü","note":"õ gives Estonian away. Finnish looks similar (ä ö, double vowels) but has no õ or ü.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=tänav"},{"n":"Eswatini","dom":".sz","side":"L","call":"+268","plate":"WHITE: Black letters over a picture of hills, e.g. JSD 136 AM (SD for Eswatini, the last letter for the region).","pos":"Eswatini: plain wooden poles with three insulators mounted on the side.","arch":"Eswatini: green hills with scattered brick houses and cement bus shelters.","ps":"txt=JSD 136 AM","lang":"English, siSwati","script":"Road · Street · MR3 · Mb- · Ng- · -eni","note":"siSwati names with Mb-, Ng-, Hl- and -eni (Ezulwini, Siteki, Lobamba). Route numbers start MR (MR3), and some old signs still say Swaziland. Greener and hillier than Botswana; Lesotho is higher and treeless.","po":"mat=wood; form=round","pot":"Eswatini: plain wooden pole with three insulators on its side.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Eswatini: southern African lines (white centre, often triple; yellow edges); green signs with yellow MR numbers; white-and-red guardrail reflectors; landline codes 24 Hhohho, 25 Manzini, 23 Lubombo, 22 Shiselweni."},{"n":"Falkland Islands","dom":".fk","side":"L","call":"+500","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: UK-style plates, white front and yellow rear.","arch":"Falkland Islands: white or bright houses with colourful tin roofs on windswept moorland.","ps":"bg=white; rear=yellow; txt=F 1234","lang":"English","script":"Road · Street · Stanley","note":"British plates (white front, yellow rear) and left-hand traffic in the South Atlantic. Trekker coverage across treeless grass, peat and rock; penguins are a strong hint.","ros":"Falkland Islands: keeps left; mostly gravel tracks with little road furniture; Trekker and boat coverage."},{"n":"Faroe Islands","dom":".fo","side":"R","call":"+298","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate, blue left band with FO and Faroese flag.","arch":"Faroe Islands: black-tarred or bright wooden houses with grass roofs on steep green slopes.","ps":"band=blue; code=FO","lang":"Faroese","script":"gøta · vegur · ð ø","note":"Faroese has both ð and ø; Icelandic has ð and þ but no ø; Danish has ø but no ð.","ro":"word=gøta / vegur","ros":"Faroe Islands: names end in -vegur or -gøta; letters ð and ø."},{"n":"Finland","dom":".fi","side":"R","call":"+358","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (FIN) and hyphenated text.","pos":"Finland: wooden poles, often with small yellow number tags, among forest and lakes.","arch":"Finland: red or yellow wooden houses with white trim set in forest.","ps":"band=eu; code=FIN","lang":"Finnish / Swedish","script":"katu · tie · ä ö · aa kk","note":"Double letters (aa, kk) with ä ö and no å in Finnish words. Bilingual towns add Swedish -gatan / -vägen; Estonian would add õ.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"Finland: simple treated wooden poles, like Sweden but usually without the yellow tag.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-yellow; word=katu / tie","ros":"Finland: street names end in -tie or -katu; bilingual Finnish / Swedish in some towns."},{"n":"France","dom":".fr","side":"R","call":"+33","plate":"DOUBLE BLUE: Long white plate with blue strips on BOTH left (F) and right (region).","pos":"France: grey concrete poles with recessed ladder-like steps and small blue ID tags.","arch":"France: stone houses with orange tile roofs in the south; slate roofs in the north.","ps":"band=eu-double; code=F","lang":"French","script":"Rue · Avenue · é è ç","note":"'Rue' also appears in Belgium, Luxembourg, Switzerland and Monaco; check the plates (blue bands on both sides) to confirm France.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=steps; tag=blue; top=crossarm","pot":"France: grey concrete with recessed ladder notches up the side and a small blue metal ID tag.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=Rue","ros":"France: 'Rue' / 'Avenue' / 'Chemin' come first; blue enamel street plaques.","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=red-ring","bot":"France: round white post with a domed top and a red reflective band wrapping all the way around.","bos":"France: round white post with a red reflective band wrapping around the top."},{"n":"Georgia","dom":".ge","side":"R","call":"+995","plate":"FLAG BAND: Long white plate with the white-red Georgian flag and GE on the left.","pos":"Georgia: Soviet-style concrete poles; yellow gas pipes run along many village roads.","arch":"Georgia: houses with large wooden balconies; tin roofs in villages.","ps":"band=flag; flag=white-red; code=GE; txt=AA-123-AA","lang":"Georgian","script":"ქუჩა · ა ბ გ · -ში","note":"Georgian (Mkhedruli) has no capital letters and very round letters (ა ბ გ). Armenian has hooks and capitals.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=ქუჩა","ros":"Georgia: rounded Georgian (Mkhedruli) script; 'Kucha' = street."},{"n":"Germany","dom":".de","side":"R","call":"+49","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (D) and round state seal.","pos":"Germany: overhead poles are uncommon; concrete or wooden in rural areas.","arch":"Germany: half-timbered houses in the centre and south, brick in the north, steep tiled roofs.","ps":"band=eu; code=D; crest=seal","lang":"German","script":"Straße · Weg · ä ö ü ß","note":"Germany and Austria write ß ('Straße'); Switzerland and Liechtenstein write 'Strasse'. Dutch 'straat' looks close but has no umlauts.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Straße / Weg","ros":"Germany: street names end in -straße, -weg or -allee and use ß.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=white-vert","bot":"Germany: white post with an angled black top and a tall vertical white reflector on the front (two small dots on the back).","bos":"Germany: white post with a slanted black top and a vertical white reflector strip."},{"n":"Ghana","dom":".gh","side":"R","call":"+233","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White (private) or yellow (commercial, very common), black text, e.g. GR 1234-20.","pos":"Ghana: wooden or concrete poles on red laterite soil. Google car has black tape on its roof rack.","arch":"Ghana: concrete houses with tin roofs; mud houses in the north.","ps":"txt=GR 1234-20","lang":"English (official), Akan (Twi), Ewe, Ga","script":"Road · Street · -krom · -man · ɛ ɔ","note":"English signs; Akan shop signs use ɛ and ɔ, and towns end in -krom, -man or -ase (Kumasi, Nkawkaw). The black tape on the Google car's front-right roof bar is unique to Ghana.","po":"mat=wood; form=round","pot":"Ghana: wooden or concrete poles on red laterite soil.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Ghana: white centre (dashed or solid), white edges mainly near towns, often no markings at all; English signs; kilometre posts naming towns; red-and-white striped posts; palms in the south, drier north.","bos":"Ghana: UK-style posts; kilometre markers naming towns; occasional red-and-white striped poles."},{"n":"Gibraltar","dom":".gi","side":"R","call":"+350","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: UK-style white front, yellow rear, with GBZ band.","ps":"band=eu; code=GBZ; rear=yellow","lang":"English","script":"Road · Street","note":"English signs with a British look, but traffic drives on the right, unlike the UK.","ros":"Gibraltar: UK-style signs but right-hand traffic; 'LOOK LEFT' painted on crossings."},{"n":"Greece","dom":".gr","side":"R","call":"+30","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (GR) and 3 letters + 4 digits.","pos":"Greece: wooden poles in the countryside; concrete in towns.","ps":"band=eu; code=GR","lang":"Greek","script":"Οδός · Λεωφόρος","note":"Greek alphabet (Λ, Σ, Ω). Bulgarian Cyrillic shares some shapes (П, Г) but adds Ж, Ъ and Щ, which Greek never uses.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-yellow; word=Οδός","ros":"Greece: Greek alphabet; 'Οδός' = street; white road lines."},{"n":"Greenland","dom":".gl","side":"R","call":"+299","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate with Greenland flag on the left.","arch":"Greenland: brightly painted wooden houses (red, blue, yellow) on rocky ground.","lang":"Greenlandic (Kalaallisut), Danish","script":"Aqqusinersuaq · qq · vej","note":"Greenlandic words are long, with qq, rs and ss and almost no æ ø (those are Danish). Road lines are all white, and yield signs stand where stop signs would be. Each town's Street View vehicle differs (ATV, boat, pickup).","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid","ros":"Greenland: all-white road lines (no yellow centre); yield signs instead of stop signs; signs mostly in Greenlandic; tiny local road networks."},{"n":"Guam","dom":".gu","side":"R","call":"+1 671","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy US-size plate reading 'GUAM'.","arch":"Guam: flat-roofed concrete typhoon-proof houses in tropical vegetation.","ps":"size=na; top=GUAM; txt=ABC 1234","lang":"English, Chamorro","script":"Route · Rt · å ñ","note":"US-style signs with Chamorro place names (Hagåtña, Dededo).","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"Guam: US-style signs and route shields; right-hand traffic; tropical villages."},{"n":"Guatemala","dom":".gt","side":"R","call":"+502","plate":"BLUE TOP: Short boxy white plate with blue top band and P-prefix (private).","pos":"Guatemala: concrete poles, most painted red at the base; medium all-green poles carry TV and internet lines.","arch":"Guatemala: colourful colonial houses in Antigua; adobe with tin roofs elsewhere.","ps":"size=na; topbar=blue; top=GUATEMALA; txt=P 123ABC","lang":"Spanish (Mayan languages widely spoken)","script":"Calle · Avenida · Zona · ALTO","note":"Spanish with a roof-rack Google car: Guatemala's rack is bare silver, the Dominican Republic's has black caps in the middle. City addresses use numbered Calle / Avenida plus Zona (Zona 1, Zona 10).","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; base=red; top=crossarm","pot":"Guatemala: concrete pole with its base painted, most often red.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Avenida","ros":"Guatemala: yellow centre, white edges; stop signs say ALTO; 'Una Vía' / 'Doble Vía' one-way signs in grid towns; Google car with a bare silver roof rack everywhere.","bos":"Guatemala: bollards are extremely rare."},{"n":"Hong Kong","dom":".hk","side":"L","call":"+852","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: UK-style white front, yellow rear, black text, no band.","pos":"Hong Kong: few poles; dense urban streets and hillsides.","arch":"Hong Kong: square three-storey village houses in the New Territories; towers elsewhere.","ps":"bg=white; txt=AB 1234; rear=yellow","lang":"Chinese (Traditional), English","script":"道 · 街 · Road · 灣","note":"Traditional characters (道, 灣) with English on every sign. Macau pairs Chinese with Portuguese (Rua, Avenida) instead.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Hong Kong: bilingual Chinese-English signs; left-hand traffic.","bos":"Hong Kong: no standard roadside posts; railings and yellow kerb lines instead."},{"n":"Hungary","dom":".hu","side":"R","call":"+36","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (H) and 3–4 letters + 3 digits.","pos":"Hungary: rectangular concrete poles with holes running all the way to the ground.","arch":"Hungary: long single-storey plastered houses set gable-end to the street.","ps":"band=eu; code=H","lang":"Hungarian","script":"utca · út · ő ű","note":"ő and ű (double acute accents) are Hungarian-only; look for sz, gy and cs clusters.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=full; top=crossarm","pot":"Hungary: rectangular concrete pole with holes running all the way to the ground.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=utca","ros":"Hungary: street names end in 'utca' (u.) or 'út'; letters ő and ű.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; cap=black; refl=red-vert","bot":"Hungary: flat-topped (not angled) white post with a black top band and a vertical red reflector."},{"n":"Iceland","dom":".is","side":"R","call":"+354","plate":"WHITE + BLUE: White plate with blue text and small blue strip (IS) on left.","pos":"Iceland: very few poles in treeless volcanic landscapes.","arch":"Iceland: houses clad in corrugated metal with colourful roofs.","ps":"band=blue; code=IS; ink=blue","lang":"Icelandic","script":"gata · vegur · þ ð æ","note":"þ (thorn) is Iceland-only. Faroese has ð but uses ø instead of þ.","ro":"warn=tri-yellow; word=gata / vegur","ros":"Iceland: letters þ and ð; names end in -gata or -vegur."},{"n":"India","dom":".in","side":"L","call":"+91","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Long or boxy; white (private) or yellow (commercial), black text.","pos":"India: concrete poles with messy cables; highway milestones also help.","arch":"India: concrete houses, often unfinished, with styles varying strongly by region.","ps":"band=blue; code=IND; txt=MH 12 AB 1234","lang":"Hindi, English, regional languages","script":"मार्ग · Marg · रोड","note":"Devanagari has a straight top bar, like Nepali; India adds English and many regional scripts (Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Bengali, Gujarati). Nepal has red plates and no English-plus-regional mix.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; cables=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"India: slim rectangular concrete poles with messy cables.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Marg","ros":"India: English plus regional scripts; yellow-topped NH milestones.","bos":"India: milestones are the clue: yellow top = National Highway, green = State Highway, orange or white = district roads."},{"n":"Indonesia","dom":".id","side":"L","call":"+62","plate":"BLACK / WHITE: Long black plate (white text) or newer white plate (black text).","pos":"Indonesia: telescoping grey steel poles made of stepped segments.","arch":"Indonesia: houses with terracotta tile hip roofs and open verandas.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=B 1234 ABC","lang":"Indonesian","script":"Jalan · Jl. · Gang · Gg.","note":"Latin script. Malaysian is almost the same, but Indonesia abbreviates 'Jl.' and 'Gg.' and has black or new white plates; Malaysia writes 'Jln' and has only black plates.","po":"mat=steel; form=segments; top=crossarm","pot":"Indonesia: telescoping black or grey steel segments that narrow towards the top.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Jalan (Jl.)","ros":"Indonesia: 'Jalan' (Jl.) on green street signs; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Iraq","dom":".iq","side":"R","call":"+964","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate with governorate name; newer plates have IRQ band. Landmark-only coverage.","arch":"Iraq: flat-roofed concrete houses behind walls; greener in the Kurdish north.","ps":"bg=white; top=بغداد BAGHDAD; txt=12345","lang":"Arabic, Kurdish (Sorani)","script":"شارع · شەقام · ە ێ ۆ","note":"Kurdish Sorani adds ە ێ ۆ ڕ ڵ, which Arabic doesn't use, so those letters point to the Kurdistan Region.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Iraq: Arabic and Kurdish signs, sometimes with English."},{"n":"Ireland","dom":".ie","side":"L","call":"+353","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (IRL) and year-county code.","pos":"Ireland: wooden poles, often in H-shaped pairs.","arch":"Ireland: white pebbledash bungalows; stone cottages in the west.","ps":"band=eu; code=IRL","lang":"English / Irish","script":"Road · Bóthar · Sráid","note":"Bilingual signs with Irish (á é í ó ú), often in italics. Northern Ireland and the rest of the UK are English-only.","po":"mat=wood; form=H; stay=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Ireland: wooden poles, often paired as a double H-pole.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-dashed; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"Ireland: bilingual Irish / English signs; dashed yellow outer lines."},{"n":"Isle of Man","dom":".im","side":"L","call":"+44","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: UK-style white front, yellow rear, with GBM band and three-legs emblem.","arch":"Isle of Man: stone cottages with slate roofs in British-style villages.","ps":"band=blue; code=GBM; rear=yellow","lang":"English / Manx","script":"Road · Straid","note":"English with occasional Manx Gaelic; otherwise it looks British."},{"n":"Israel","dom":".il","side":"R","call":"+972","plate":"YELLOW + BLUE: Long yellow plate with blue left strip (IL + flag).","pos":"Israel: concrete poles in dry, sunny landscapes.","arch":"Israel: white or cream stone-clad buildings with flat roofs and solar heaters.","ps":"bg=yellow; band=blue; code=IL; txt=123-45-678","lang":"Hebrew, Arabic, English","script":"רחוב · שדרות · א ב ג","note":"Square Hebrew letters read right to left, stacked with Arabic and English. Palestinian signs are Arabic-English, without Hebrew.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=רחוב","ros":"Israel: trilingual Hebrew-Arabic-English signs; kerbs painted red-white (no stopping) or blue-white (paid parking); yellow plates."},{"n":"Italy","dom":".it","side":"R","call":"+39","plate":"DOUBLE BLUE: Long white plate with blue EU strips on BOTH left (I) and right; small front.","pos":"Italy: concrete poles; many cables run along building walls.","arch":"Italy: stucco houses with terracotta roofs and wooden shutters.","ps":"band=eu-double; code=I","lang":"Italian","script":"Via · Piazza · Corso","note":"'Via' is also used in San Marino and Ticino (Switzerland). Italian words end in vowels and only use accents like à è.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Via","ros":"Italy: 'Via', 'Corso', 'Piazza'; white road lines.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; cap=black-third; refl=red-rect","bot":"Italy: white post with the top third painted black and a red rectangle on the right side (white on the left)."},{"n":"Japan","dom":".jp","side":"L","call":"+81","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Short boxy plate; white (green text) or yellow (black text, kei cars).","pos":"Japan: concrete poles with yellow-black stripe plates that stop above the ground; dense wiring.","arch":"Japan: tidy two-storey houses with grey or blue glazed tile roofs and panel cladding; tiny kei cars in driveways.","ps":"bg=white; ink=green; size=short; top=品川 300; txt=さ 12-34","lang":"Japanese","script":"通り · の · ア · 町","note":"Kana (の, さ, ア) exist only in Japanese. Chinese has only Han characters; Korean Hangul uses circles and boxes (한).","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; stripes=vertical-plate; bolts=yes; top=crossarm; cables=yes","pot":"Japan: grey concrete pole with climbing bolts and a yellow-black plate that stops above the ground; dense wiring.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"Japan: kana and kanji on signs; white edge lines and narrow roads.","bo":"body=grey; top=disc; refl=disc; thin=yes","bot":"Japan: thin post topped with a large round reflector disc.","bos":"Japan: posts and walls with round yellow reflectors along roads."},{"n":"Jersey","dom":".je","side":"L","call":"+44","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: UK-style white front, yellow rear, J prefix.","arch":"Jersey: granite stone houses with slate roofs, a British-French mix.","ps":"band=none; rear=yellow","lang":"English (French names)","script":"Road · Rue","note":"English signs, but many streets keep French 'Rue' names, an unusual mix.","ros":"Jersey: French street names ('Rue de…') on UK-style signs; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Jordan","dom":".jo","side":"R","call":"+962","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate with black Arabic and Western numerals.","arch":"Jordan: square limestone houses with flat roofs on dense hillsides (Amman).","ps":"bg=white; txt=12-34567; sub=JORDAN الأردن","lang":"Arabic, English","script":"شارع · Street · ع غ","note":"Arabic with English transliteration on blue street signs. Lebanon adds French (Rue).","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Jordan: bilingual Arabic-English street signs."},{"n":"Kazakhstan","dom":".kz","side":"R","call":"+7","plate":"WHITE + FLAG: Long white plate with blue flag/KZ on left and boxed region on right.","pos":"Kazakhstan: concrete poles across vast flat steppe.","arch":"Kazakhstan: low houses with tin roofs; Soviet apartment blocks in towns.","ps":"band=flag; flag=teal-teal; code=KZ; txt=123 ABC 02","lang":"Kazakh, Russian","script":"көшесі · ә ғ қ ң ө ұ ү һ і","note":"Kazakh Cyrillic adds ә ғ қ ң ө ұ ү һ і. Kyrgyz adds only ң ө ү, Mongolian ө ү. Russian-only signs (ул.) are also common.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; strut=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Kazakhstan: Soviet concrete poles, often propped by a diagonal strut.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=көшесі","ros":"Kazakhstan: Kazakh Cyrillic letters ә ғ қ ң ө ұ ү; 'көшесі' = street.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; band=black-diag; refl=red-rect","bot":"Kazakhstan: Soviet-standard white post with a black diagonal stripe, as in Russia.","bos":"Kazakhstan: Soviet-standard white posts with a black diagonal stripe, as in Russia."},{"n":"Kenya","dom":".ke","side":"L","call":"+254","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Long white front plate, short yellow rear plate, black text: three letters, three digits, a letter (KDA 123A).","pos":"Kenya: wooden poles and red soil. Google car often has a black snorkel.","arch":"Kenya: huts and simple concrete houses with tin roofs.","ps":"rear=yellow; txt=KDA 123A","lang":"Swahili, English","script":"Road · Barabara · Mtaa · Karibu","note":"Swahili (Barabara, Mtaa, Karibu) is shared with Tanzania and Uganda. Kenya: square yellow rear plates (KDA 123A) and the Google car's snorkel. Uganda: Luganda names (Ka-, Na-, Bu-) and a black bull bar on the car. Rwanda drives on the right.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"Kenya: wooden poles on red soil; solar street lights on new roads.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Kenya: yellow centre line (dashed or solid), white edges; English signs; kilometre markers; coverage mostly in the south (Nairobi, Mombasa, the Rift Valley); phone codes 07xx, 01xx.","bos":"Kenya: black-and-white striped bollards at junctions in Nairobi and Mombasa; kilometre markers on main roads."},{"n":"Kosovo","dom":".xk","side":"R","call":"+383","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (RKS) and 2-digit region code.","arch":"Kosovo: large red-brick family houses, often unfinished.","ps":"band=blue; code=RKS","lang":"Albanian / Serbian","script":"Rruga · Ulica","note":"Albanian 'Rruga' often paired with Serbian Latin 'Ulica'; Albania itself rarely adds Serbian.","ro":"word=Rruga","ros":"Kosovo: Albanian 'Rruga' signs, sometimes with Serbian."},{"n":"Kyrgyzstan","dom":".kg","side":"R","call":"+996","plate":"RED BAND: Long white plate with red left strip and flag emblem.","pos":"Kyrgyzstan: Soviet-style concrete poles below big mountains; yellow gas pipes in villages.","arch":"Kyrgyzstan: single-storey houses with tin roofs; yurts in the mountains.","ps":"band=red; code=KG; txt=01 123 ABC","lang":"Kyrgyz, Russian","script":"көчөсү · ң ө ү · ул.","note":"Kyrgyz Cyrillic adds only ң ө ү; Kazakh has more extra letters (ә ғ қ ұ і). 'көчөсү' = street.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=көчөсү","ros":"Kyrgyzstan: Kyrgyz Cyrillic with ң ү ө; 'көчөсү' = street.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; band=black-diag; refl=red-rect","bot":"Kyrgyzstan: Soviet-standard white post with a black diagonal stripe."},{"n":"Laos","dom":".la","side":"R","call":"+856","plate":"YELLOW: Short boxy yellow plate with black Lao script and numbers.","pos":"Laos: round concrete poles with far fewer cables than Thailand; wooden poles in villages.","arch":"Laos: wooden stilt houses; French colonial buildings in towns.","ps":"bg=yellow; size=short; top=ນະຄອນຫຼວງ; txt=ກຂ 1234","lang":"Lao","script":"ຖະໜົນ · ກ ຂ ຄ · ບ້ານ","note":"Lao letters are rounder than Thai, with fewer loops and no tall tails; 'Ban' (ບ້ານ, village) appears on many signs. Thai has small loops at the start of letters.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"Laos: round concrete poles with far fewer cables than Thailand.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=ຖະໜົນ","ros":"Laos: quiet two-lane roads through hills and rice fields; Lao signs with English; red-topped kilometre stones; right-hand traffic.","bos":"Laos: no standard roadside posts; white concrete kilometre stones with a red top on national roads."},{"n":"Latvia","dom":".lv","side":"R","call":"+371","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (LV) and 2 letters + numbers.","pos":"Latvia: wooden or concrete poles on flat, forested land.","arch":"Latvia: wooden houses; Soviet-era blocks in towns.","ps":"band=eu; code=LV","lang":"Latvian","script":"iela · ā ē ī ū · ķ ļ ņ","note":"Macrons (ā ē ī ū) and comma-consonants (ķ ļ ņ) are Latvian; Lithuanian uses ą ę ė į ų instead.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=iela","ros":"Latvia: macrons ā ē ī ū; 'iela' = street."},{"n":"Lebanon","dom":".lb","side":"R","call":"+961","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (cedar tree emblem).","arch":"Lebanon: sandstone houses with red tile roofs in the mountains.","ps":"band=blue; txt=B 123456","lang":"Arabic, French, English","script":"شارع · Rue · Route","note":"Arabic with French (Rue) or English is typical of Lebanon; Jordan pairs Arabic with English only.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Lebanon: Arabic with French or English; Beirut street signs show sector numbers."},{"n":"Lesotho","dom":".ls","side":"L","call":"+266","plate":"WHITE: White plate with blue characters (red for government): one or two letters, then four digits.","arch":"Lesotho: round stone huts (rondavels) with thatched roofs.","ps":"ink=blue; crest=yes; txt=A 1234","lang":"Sesotho, English","script":"Road · Ha · Thaba- · Ma- · Mo-","note":"Sesotho villages named 'Ha …' (Ha Abia) and Thaba- (mountain). Treeless highlands with flat-topped hills and people in Basotho blankets; national roads A1 to A4, though the number is often missing from signs.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Lesotho: white centre, yellow edges; green signs with yellow A numbers (often left off); taxis with a yellow side stripe; phone codes 22 and 28 (fixed), 5x and 6x (mobile).","bos":"Lesotho: very rare white rectangular posts with a black base strip and black numbers; stick markers on dirt roads."},{"n":"Liechtenstein","dom":".li","side":"R","call":"+423","plate":"BLACK: Black plates with white text starting with 'FL' (Swiss size).","arch":"Liechtenstein: Alpine houses very similar to Switzerland and Austria.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; band=none; crest=yes; size=short","lang":"German","script":"Strasse · ä ö ü","note":"Swiss-style German without ß ('Strasse'); Germany and Austria write 'Straße'.","ro":"word=Strasse","ros":"Liechtenstein: German '-strasse' without ß, as in Switzerland."},{"n":"Lithuania","dom":".lt","side":"R","call":"+370","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (LT) and 3 letters + 3 digits.","pos":"Lithuania: concrete and wooden poles across flat countryside.","arch":"Lithuania: wooden houses with steep roofs alongside new brick homes.","ps":"band=eu; code=LT","lang":"Lithuanian","script":"gatvė (g.) · ą č ę ė į š ų ž","note":"ė, į and ų are Lithuanian; Latvian uses macrons (ā ē) instead. Polish shares ą ę but adds ł ż ś.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=gatvė (g.)","ros":"Lithuania: letters ą ę į ų ė; 'gatvė' (g.) = street."},{"n":"Luxembourg","dom":".lu","side":"R","call":"+352","plate":"YELLOW + BLUE: Bright yellow plates front and rear with blue EU strip (L).","arch":"Luxembourg: plastered houses with dark slate roofs.","ps":"bg=yellow; band=eu; code=L","lang":"Luxembourgish / French / German","script":"Rue · Strooss · ë","note":"Street signs are mostly French 'Rue', with Luxembourgish names (ë); German appears too.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=Rue","ros":"Luxembourg: French 'Rue' with German / Luxembourgish names; trilingual signs.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=white-vert","bot":"Luxembourg: white post with an angled black top and a tall vertical white reflector on the front (two small dots on the back)."},{"n":"Macau","dom":".mo","side":"L","call":"+853","plate":"BLACK: Black plate with white text starting with M.","arch":"Macau: pastel Portuguese colonial façades and tiled squares next to dense high-rises and casinos.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=MA-12-34","lang":"Chinese (Traditional), Portuguese","script":"Rua · Avenida · 街 · 大馬路","note":"Portuguese street names (Rua, Avenida, Travessa) next to Traditional Chinese are unique to Macau.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Rua","ros":"Macau: Chinese-Portuguese street names on blue-and-white tiles; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Madagascar","dom":".mg","side":"R","call":"+261","plate":"WHITE: White plate, black text: four digits and three letters, the first letter for the region (1234 TBA, T = Antananarivo).","arch":"Madagascar: highland brick houses with steep roofs and wooden balconies.","ps":"txt=1234 TBA","lang":"Malagasy, French","script":"Lalana · Arabe · Antana- · Ambo- · Fianar-","note":"Malagasy words are long and vowel-heavy (Antananarivo, Ambohimanga) and never use c, q, u, w or x. Coverage is trekker, boat and a short stretch of car on the RN8 (Avenue of the Baobabs).","ro":"word=Lalana","ros":"Madagascar: mostly trekker and boat coverage; baobabs near Morondava on the RN8; poles only in the larger towns; phone numbers 020 (fixed), 032 / 033 / 034 (mobile)."},{"n":"Malaysia","dom":".my","side":"L","call":"+60","plate":"BLACK: Black plate with white characters (front and rear).","pos":"Malaysia: concrete poles, sometimes black with white stickers.","arch":"Malaysia: terrace houses in towns; wooden kampung houses on stilts.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=WXY 1234","lang":"Malay","script":"Jalan · Jln · Lebuhraya · Kg.","note":"Malay spells 'teksi', 'polis' and writes 'Jln' and 'Lebuhraya'; Indonesian prefers 'Jl.' and 'Tol'. Malaysian plates are always black.","po":"mat=black; form=round; tag=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Malaysia: black pole with a white rectangular sticker.","ro":"warn=diamond-yellow; word=Jalan","ros":"Malaysia: 'Jalan' (Jln) on street signs; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Maldives","dom":".mv","side":"L","call":"+960","plate":"WHITE: White plate with black text; landmark-only coverage.","pos":"Maldives: almost no overhead poles; white street lamps on short posts.","arch":"Maldives: low coral-stone or concrete houses on tiny islands.","ps":"bg=white; txt=C1234","lang":"Dhivehi, English","script":"މަގު · Magu · ހ ށ ނ","note":"Thaana is written right to left with small vowel marks above and below the letters; 'Magu' = street, 'Hingun' = avenue.","ro":"warn=tri-white; word=Magu","ros":"Maldives: tiny islands with sandy or paved streets crowded with scooters; signs in Dhivehi (Thaana) and English ('Magu', 'Hingun').","bos":"Maldives: no roadside posts; coral-stone kerbs and white walls instead."},{"n":"Mali","dom":".ml","side":"R","call":"+223","plate":"WHITE: Long white plate with black text; limited coverage.","arch":"Mali: mud-brick (adobe) buildings in Sahel landscapes.","lang":"French (official), Bambara","script":"Rue · Avenue · Grande Mosquée","note":"Trekker only: the mud-brick mosques of Djenné, Mopti, Niono and Timbuktu, the Askia tomb in Gao and the cliff villages near Mopti. French is the working language.","ros":"Mali: no road coverage; trekker spots at the great mosques and in the Dogon cliffs."},{"n":"Malta","dom":".mt","side":"L","call":"+356","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (M) and 3 letters + 3 digits.","arch":"Malta: limestone houses with flat roofs and enclosed wooden balconies.","ps":"band=eu; code=M","lang":"Maltese / English","script":"Triq · ħ ġ ż ċ","note":"ħ, ġ and ċ are unique to Maltese; 'Triq' (street) is a sure giveaway.","ro":"word=Triq","ros":"Malta: 'Triq' = street; letters ċ ġ ħ ż."},{"n":"Martinique","dom":".mq","side":"R","call":"+596","plate":"DOUBLE BLUE: French plate, blue bands on both sides; right band shows 972.","arch":"Martinique: Creole wooden houses with colourful shutters.","ps":"band=eu-double; code=F","lang":"French (Antillean Creole)","script":"Rue · Route · Chemin","note":"French plates (972 on the right band) and French signs in the Caribbean; Trekker-only coverage. Réunion is the French look-alike in the Indian Ocean (974); Saint-Pierre and Miquelon is cold and North Atlantic.","ro":"word=Rue","ros":"Martinique: French 'Rue' on French-style blue plaques.","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=red-ring","bot":"Martinique: round white post with a domed top and a red reflective band wrapping all the way around."},{"n":"Mexico","dom":".mx","side":"R","call":"+52","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy white plate with colored top/bottom state banners.","pos":"Mexico: smooth octagonal concrete poles.","arch":"Mexico: flat-roofed concrete houses with rebar sticking up and painted walls.","ps":"size=na; top=JALISCO; txt=JKL-123-A","lang":"Spanish","script":"Calle · Colonia (Col.) · ALTO · Tope","note":"ALTO on stop signs is shared with Guatemala and Costa Rica; most of South America writes PARE. Nahuatl place names (-tepec, -tlán, many x) and smooth octagonal concrete poles point to Mexico.","po":"mat=concrete; form=octagonal; top=crossarm; transformer=yes","pot":"Mexico: smooth tapered octagonal concrete pole with no holes, horizontal crossarms and cylindrical transformers.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Mexico: yellow centre lines (double on major roads), white edges; stop signs say ALTO; yellow warning signs with large bold pictures; 'Tope' speed-bump signs; kilometre markers; Calle and Colonia (Col.).","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=white-vert; foot=black","bot":"Mexico: round white post with a black lower section and a yellow back, like a cigarette; common in the north and the south.","bos":"Mexico: round white bollard with a black base and a yellow back (cigarette-like), common in the north and south."},{"n":"Midway Atoll","dom":".us","side":"R","call":"+1 808","plate":"STANDARD NA: US-style plate.","lang":"English","script":"Road · Midway","note":"US wildlife refuge on a remote atoll; English signs only.","code":"UM"},{"n":"Monaco","dom":".mc","side":"R","call":"+377","plate":"WHITE + BLUE: Compact white plate with blue characters and Monaco shield on left.","arch":"Monaco: pastel Mediterranean apartment buildings packed on the hillside.","ps":"band=none; ink=blue; crest=yes; size=short","lang":"French","script":"Rue · Avenue · Boulevard","note":"Same French as France; tell them apart by Monaco's compact white plates and dense coastal city.","ro":"word=Rue","ros":"Monaco: bilingual French and Monégasque street signs."},{"n":"Mongolia","dom":".mn","side":"R","call":"+976","plate":"WHITE + RED: Long white plate with red Soyombo symbol on the left.","pos":"Mongolia: concrete poles across open steppe. Google car carries camping gear on its roof.","arch":"Mongolia: white gers (yurts) beside small houses with colourful green, red or blue metal roofs, behind wooden fences.","ps":"band=flag; flag=red-blue-red; code=MNG; txt=1234 УБА","lang":"Mongolian","script":"гудамж · ө ү · Улаанбаатар","note":"Mongolian Cyrillic adds only ө and ү and doubles vowels (аа, үү). Kazakh and Kyrgyz add more letters.","ro":"warn=tri-white; word=гудамж","ros":"Mongolia: Mongolian Cyrillic with ө and ү."},{"n":"Montenegro","dom":".me","side":"R","call":"+382","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (MNE) and round gold/red crest.","arch":"Montenegro: stone houses with red tile roofs in coastal towns.","ps":"band=blue; code=MNE; crest=yes","lang":"Montenegrin","script":"ulica · улица · ś ź","note":"Latin and Cyrillic like Serbia; Montenegrin spelling can add ś and ź.","ro":"word=ulica","ros":"Montenegro: Latin and Cyrillic street signs ('Ulica')."},{"n":"Namibia","dom":".na","side":"L","call":"+264","plate":"YELLOW: Yellow plates front and rear, black text: N, a number, then the town letters (N 12345 W is Windhoek, S Swakopmund, WB Walvis Bay).","pos":"Namibia: wooden poles in open desert; yellow lamp posts in Swakopmund and Henties Bay.","arch":"Namibia: colourful town houses set against desert landscapes.","ps":"bg=yellow; txt=N 12345 W","lang":"English (official), Afrikaans, German, Oshiwambo","script":"Street · Straat · Straße · B1 · C34","note":"German street and town names (Swakopmund, Lüderitz, -straße) are unique in Africa. Roads are lettered A to D (B1, C34); South Africa uses N, R and M. Plates are yellow front and rear.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Street / Straat","ros":"Namibia: white centre, yellow edges (missing or faded on parts of the B1 and in the north); green direction signs with yellow route numbers; A, B, C and D roads; Windhoek's street signs are plain white.","bos":"Namibia: small stone bollards at junctions (German colonial); on the C34 coast road, white posts with two yellow reflectors in front and one red behind."},{"n":"Nepal","dom":".np","side":"L","call":"+977","plate":"RED / WHITE: Red plate with white text (private) or black/white; Nepali script.","pos":"Nepal: concrete poles with dense wiring in Kathmandu.","arch":"Nepal: brick houses with carved wooden windows; stone houses in the mountains.","ps":"bg=red; ink=white; size=short; top=बा १ च; txt=१२३४","lang":"Nepali","script":"मार्ग · सडक · ने पा ल","note":"Same Devanagari as Hindi, but Nepali signs rarely add other scripts; red plates with white Devanagari are typical of Nepal.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Marg","ros":"Nepal: Devanagari script; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Netherlands","dom":".nl","side":"R","call":"+31","plate":"YELLOW + BLUE: Bright yellow plates front and rear with blue EU strip (NL).","pos":"Netherlands: overhead poles are rare because most cables are underground.","arch":"Netherlands: red-brown brick terraced houses with large uncurtained windows.","ps":"bg=yellow; band=eu; code=NL","lang":"Dutch","script":"straat · weg · laan · ij","note":"Look for 'ij' and double vowels (aa ee oo). Flanders in Belgium uses the same words; German would show ä ö ü.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=straat / weg","bos":"Netherlands: white roadside post with a green hectometre number plate."},{"n":"New Zealand","dom":".nz","side":"L","call":"+64","plate":"WHITE: Medium slim white plate with plain black characters and no border.","pos":"New Zealand: concrete poles, sometimes square with holes.","arch":"New Zealand: weatherboard timber houses with corrugated iron roofs; Māori carved meeting houses (wharenui) in some villages.","ps":"bg=white; txt=ABC123","lang":"English, Māori","script":"Rd · SH · Wh · ā ō","note":"Māori place names (Wh-, Ng-, macrons ā ō) and 'SH' state highway shields; Australia has no Māori words.","po":"mat=concrete; form=square; holes=square-holes; top=crossarm","pot":"New Zealand: square concrete poles with holes.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"New Zealand: English and Māori names; white road lines.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; refl=red-wrap","bot":"New Zealand: white post with a wider red reflector band wrapping around the edges."},{"n":"Nigeria","dom":".ng","side":"R","call":"+234","plate":"WHITE: North American size; white plate with green and blue text, state name at the top and slogan at the bottom (ABC 123 DE).","pos":"Nigeria: many ladder-style concrete poles, double poles, and \"broken\" poles; light poles in cities.","arch":"Nigeria: concrete houses with tin roofs.","ps":"size=na; ink=green; top=LAGOS; txt=ABC 123 DE; sub=CENTRE OF EXCELLENCE","lang":"English (official), Hausa, Yoruba, Igbo","script":"Road · Close · Crescent · ọ ẹ ṣ","note":"English with British road words (Close, Crescent). Yoruba names with dotted ọ ẹ ṣ in the south-west, Hausa (ƙ ɗ) in the north. A police pickup or SUV following the Google car is Nigeria's giveaway.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=ladder","pot":"Nigeria: ladder-style concrete poles, often doubled; many look broken or patched.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road / Close","ros":"Nigeria: lines mostly white (yellow edges are rare); street-sign colours change by area (Lekki and Victoria Island red, Abuja bordered arrows); green-and-white painted concrete dividers; Lagos taxis yellow with two black stripes; Abuja phone code 09."},{"n":"North Macedonia","dom":".mk","side":"R","call":"+389","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (NMK) and small red box.","pos":"North Macedonia: concrete poles, often with stork nests in villages.","arch":"North Macedonia: stone or brick houses; Ottoman-style homes in old towns.","ps":"band=blue; code=NMK","lang":"Macedonian / Albanian","script":"улица · ѓ ќ ѕ љ њ","note":"Macedonian Cyrillic has ѓ ќ ѕ; Serbian Cyrillic uses ђ ћ instead; Bulgarian uses ъ and щ.","ro":"word=улица","ros":"North Macedonia: Macedonian Cyrillic with ѓ ќ ѕ."},{"n":"Northern Mariana Islands","dom":".mp","side":"R","call":"+1 670","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy US-size plate.","arch":"Northern Mariana Islands: flat-roofed concrete typhoon-proof houses, like Guam.","ps":"size=na; top=CNMI SAIPAN; txt=ABC 123","lang":"English, Chamorro, Carolinian","script":"Road · Beach Road · å","note":"US-style signs with Chamorro and Carolinian names, like Guam.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow"},{"n":"Norway","dom":".no","side":"R","call":"+47","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue strip (N + flag); green for commercial vans.","arch":"Norway: wooden houses painted red, white or yellow on steep fjord slopes.","ps":"band=blue; code=N","lang":"Norwegian","script":"vei · gate · æ ø å","note":"Same letters as Danish; Norwegian 'vei / veien', Danish 'vej'. Swedish uses ä ö.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=gata / veien","ros":"Norway: street names end in -vegen / -veien or -gata; yellow centre lines."},{"n":"Oman","dom":".om","side":"R","call":"+968","plate":"YELLOW / RED: Yellow (private) or red (commercial) plates with Arabic/English text.","arch":"Oman: flat-roofed white or beige houses behind walls.","ps":"bg=yellow; top=عمان OMAN; txt=1234 AB","lang":"Arabic, English","script":"شارع · Way · Street","note":"Oman numbers its streets as 'Way 1234' on bilingual signs; UAE and Qatar use 'Street' / 'St'.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Oman: bilingual Arabic-English signs."},{"n":"Pakistan","dom":".pk","side":"L","call":"+92","plate":"WHITE: Mostly white plates with black text; design varies by province.","pos":"Pakistan: concrete or steel poles with tangled wiring in towns.","arch":"Pakistan: brick or mud houses with flat roofs.","ps":"bg=white; top=PUNJAB; txt=LEA 1234","lang":"Urdu, English","script":"روڈ · سڑک · ٹ ڈ ڑ ے","note":"Urdu uses the Arabic script with extra letters ٹ ڈ ڑ ے; signs are often English first with Urdu below.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Pakistan: colourful decorated trucks (truck art), green motorway signs, English-Urdu signs; left-hand traffic."},{"n":"Palestine","dom":".ps","side":"R","call":"+970","plate":"WHITE: White plate with black text and Palestine marking; Israeli yellow plates also seen.","pos":"Palestine: concrete poles in hilly olive-grove country.","arch":"Palestine: flat-roofed houses of pale limestone with black or white rooftop water tanks on terraced hills.","ps":"bg=white; top=فلسطين P; txt=7-1234-95","lang":"Arabic, English","script":"شارع · Street · ق ص","note":"Arabic-English signs without Hebrew; Israeli signs add Hebrew. Palestinian plates are white, Israeli ones yellow.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Palestine: Arabic-English signs."},{"n":"Panama","dom":".pa","side":"R","call":"+507","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate with colorful graphic strip (rear-only on many cars).","pos":"Panama: concrete poles in tropical surroundings.","arch":"Panama: wooden stilt houses in Bocas del Toro; concrete elsewhere.","ps":"size=na; top=PANAMÁ; txt=AB1234","lang":"Spanish","script":"Calle · Vía · Corregimiento · PARE","note":"PARE on stop signs, unlike ALTO in Costa Rica to the west. Plates are rear-only on many cars, and the Google car is a pickup with an antenna near the front.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Vía","ros":"Panama: single yellow centre line (double is rare), white edges; kilometre markers on rural roads; stop signs say PARE; round metal sign poles; colourful satellite dishes (Claro, TV Digital).","bos":"Panama: no official bollards; kilometre markers on rural roads instead."},{"n":"Paraguay","dom":".py","side":"R","call":"+595","plate":"MERCOSUR / WHITE: Long white plate with blue Mercosur top band or older white-red plate.","pos":"Paraguay: concrete poles beside red soil.","arch":"Paraguay: brick houses with tile roofs and colonial verandas.","ps":"topbar=blue; top=PARAGUAY; txt=ABCD 123","lang":"Spanish, Guaraní","script":"Calle · Ruta PY01 · ñ · ỹ","note":"Guaraní words with ỹ, ẽ and the puso (') appear on signs. Red soil like southern Brazil, but in Spanish; national routes were renumbered PY01–PY22 in 2019.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Paraguay: yellow centre, white edges; black or grey sign backs; some Portuguese signs near the Itaipú dam; national routes PY01–PY22."},{"n":"Peru","dom":".pe","side":"R","call":"+51","plate":"WHITE + FLAG: Short boxy white plate with top stripe (yellow/orange for taxis).","pos":"Peru: concrete poles with painted bottoms (most often black); some with three arms sticking out.","arch":"Peru: adobe houses in the Andes; wooden houses in the Amazon.","ps":"size=na; topbar=red; top=PERÚ; txt=ABC-123","lang":"Spanish, Quechua, Aymara","script":"Jirón (Jr.) · Calle · Pasaje · PARE","note":"The street word 'Jirón (Jr.)' is only Peruvian. Mototaxis (three-wheelers) and election slogans painted on walls ('Alcalde') are strong Peru clues against Bolivia and Ecuador.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; base=black; top=crossarm","pot":"Peru: concrete pole with the bottom painted, most often black.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Jirón / Calle","ros":"Peru: yellow centre on two-lane roads, white on divided highways; black-and-white striped sign posts set in concrete blocks; stop signs say PARE; mototaxis; 'PE-1N' style national routes.","bo":"body=white; top=round; cap=black-band","bot":"Peru: round \"cigarette\" bollard, plain or with black stripes; Moquegua has a black-and-yellow top.","bos":"Peru: round cigarette-like bollards, plain or black-striped; Moquegua region has its own black-and-yellow topped bollard."},{"n":"Philippines","dom":".ph","side":"R","call":"+63","plate":"WHITE / GREEN: North American size; white or older green-tinted mountain design.","pos":"Philippines: concrete poles with very messy wiring.","arch":"Philippines: concrete houses with corrugated metal roofs, sari-sari stores and basketball hoops everywhere.","ps":"size=na; top=PILIPINAS; txt=ABC 1234","lang":"English, Filipino (Tagalog)","script":"Street · Kalye · Barangay · Daan","note":"English signs with Tagalog words (Barangay, Purok, Sari-Sari Store). Jeepneys and tricycles are unique to the Philippines.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"Philippines: English signs; jeepneys and tricycles on the road."},{"n":"Pitcairn Islands","dom":".pn","side":"L","call":"+64","plate":"NONE: Very few vehicles; no standard plate to learn.","arch":"Pitcairn Islands: a few timber houses with tin roofs on steep green slopes; red dirt tracks.","lang":"English, Pitkern","script":"Road · Adamstown","note":"Only Adamstown: red dirt tracks and quad bikes; English and Pitkern names."},{"n":"Poland","dom":".pl","side":"R","call":"+48","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (PL) and 2–3 letter region prefix.","pos":"Poland: concrete poles with long rectangular slots that stop above the base.","arch":"Poland: wooden highland houses (Zakopane style); plastered modern houses elsewhere.","ps":"band=eu; code=PL","lang":"Polish","script":"ulica (ul.) · ł ż ś ź ć ń ą ę","note":"ł, ż and ś are Polish-only. Czech 'ulice' has ř and ě; Slovak and Croatian 'ulica' have no ł or ż.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=slots; top=crossarm","pot":"Poland: concrete pole with long narrow slots that stop well above the bottom; often paired as an A-frame.","ro":"warn=tri-yellow; word=ulica (ul.)","ros":"Poland: 'ul.' (ulica) on street signs; white lines.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=red-band; num=yes","bot":"Poland: white post with an angled black top, a red stripe across the middle and small black route numbers."},{"n":"Portugal","dom":".pt","side":"R","call":"+351","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (P); older plates have yellow right band.","ps":"band=eu; code=P","lang":"Portuguese","script":"Rua · Avenida · ã õ ç","note":"Portuguese 'Rua' with ã and õ; Spanish writes 'Calle' and ñ, Catalan 'Carrer', Galician 'Rúa'.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=ladder; top=crossarm","pot":"Portugal: concrete with smaller, tighter ladder notches than France.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Rua","ros":"Portugal: 'Rua' / 'Avenida', often on blue-and-white azulejo tiles."},{"n":"Puerto Rico","dom":".pr","side":"R","call":"+1","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy white plate reading 'PUERTO RICO'.","pos":"Puerto Rico: Mexican-style octagonal concrete poles and large square ones beside US-style roads.","ps":"size=na; top=PUERTO RICO; txt=ABC-123; sub=ISLA DEL ENCANTO","lang":"Spanish, English","script":"Calle · Carr. · PARE · km","note":"US-style roads and signs, but in Spanish: stop signs say PARE, distances are in km while speed limits stay in mph. Plates are rear-only.","po":"mat=concrete; form=octagonal; top=crossarm","pot":"Puerto Rico: Mexican-style octagonal concrete pole; large square concrete poles too.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle","ros":"Puerto Rico: US-style signs in Spanish; stop signs say PARE; kilometre markers but speed limits in mph; 'Carr.' / PR- route shields; white Google car with an antenna on the front passenger side."},{"n":"Qatar","dom":".qa","side":"R","call":"+974","plate":"MAROON BAND: Long white plate with maroon left strip and 'QATAR'.","arch":"Qatar: flat-roofed stone or concrete villas behind walls.","ps":"band=maroon; code=QATAR; txt=123456","lang":"Arabic, English","script":"شارع · St · Zone","note":"Blue Qatari street signs show a street number and a zone number; plates carry QATAR in a maroon band.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"Qatar: bilingual Arabic-English street signs."},{"n":"Réunion","dom":".re","side":"R","call":"+262","plate":"DOUBLE BLUE: French plate, blue bands on both sides; right band shows 974.","pos":"Réunion: mainland French pole styles across the island.","arch":"Réunion: Creole wooden houses (cases) with colourful verandas.","ps":"band=eu-double; code=F; dept=974; txt=AB-123-CD","lang":"French, Réunion Creole","script":"Rue · Chemin · Ravine · Îlet · Saint-","note":"French like the mainland, but tropical and volcanic. Plates show 974 in the right band; Creole place names (Ravine, Îlet, Bras-) and many Saint- towns. Martinique has 972 and Caribbean scenery.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=steps; tag=blue; top=crossarm","pot":"Réunion: the same concrete poles as mainland France.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=Rue / Chemin","ros":"Réunion: dashed white centre and edges; D roads common, few N roads (the N3 crosses the mountains); blue-and-white corner arrows; phone numbers 0262 (fixed), 0692 / 0693 (mobile).","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=red-ring","bot":"Réunion: round white post with a domed top and a red reflective band wrapping all the way around.","bos":"Réunion: road markers off the road or on medians; mainland French bollards are uncommon; yellow-capped kilometre markers."},{"n":"Romania","dom":".ro","side":"R","call":"+40","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (RO) and county code prefix.","pos":"Romania: concrete poles with holes that stop above a solid base, often white-painted at the bottom.","arch":"Romania: houses with ornate metal roofs, big gates and painted walls.","ps":"band=eu; code=RO","lang":"Romanian","script":"Strada (Str.) · ă â î ș ț","note":"ă, ș and ț are Romanian; 'Strada' looks Italian, but Italian never uses those letters.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=above-base; base=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Romania: holey concrete like Hungary, but the holes stop above a solid base that is often painted white.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Strada","ros":"Romania: 'Strada' (Str.); letters ș ț ă."},{"n":"Russia","dom":".ru","side":"R","call":"+7","plate":"WHITE + BOX: Long white plate with separated right-side box for region code.","pos":"Russia: concrete poles with a diagonal support strut and white-painted base.","arch":"Russia: wooden izba houses with painted window frames; Soviet blocks in towns.","ps":"band=none; rbox=yes","lang":"Russian","script":"улица (ул.) · ы э ё","note":"ы and э appear in Russian (and Belarusian) only; Ukrainian has і ї є, Bulgarian has ъ щ.","po":"mat=concrete; form=octagonal; strut=yes; base=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Russia: octagonal grey concrete pole held up by a diagonal concrete strut, with a white-painted base.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=улица (ул.)","ros":"Russia: Russian Cyrillic (ы э ё); 'ул.' = street.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; band=black-diag; refl=red-rect","bot":"Russia: white post with a diagonal black stripe across the upper half and a small red-white reflector inside it.","bos":"Russia: white post with a black band near the top and a reflector; Soviet-standard design also used in Belarus and Kazakhstan."},{"n":"Rwanda","dom":".rw","side":"R","call":"+250","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear, black text starting with R (RAD 123 A).","pos":"Rwanda: round or square tapering poles, junction poles, and light poles with blue bottoms on major roads.","arch":"Rwanda: brick houses with tin roofs on terraced hillsides.","ps":"rear=yellow; txt=RAD 123 A","lang":"Kinyarwanda, English, French, Swahili","script":"Umuhanda · KN 5 Rd · KG · Murakaza neza","note":"Kinyarwanda words with umu-, aba-, -rwa; Kigali street codes KN, KG and KK (KN 5 Rd) on almost every street. Rwanda drives on the RIGHT, unlike Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania next door.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round","pot":"Rwanda: tapering round or square concrete poles; light poles with blue bases on main roads.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Umuhanda","ros":"Rwanda: single yellow centre line (solid or dashed) with white edges; black-and-white kerbs; street-name signs everywhere; motorcycle taxis with red vests."},{"n":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon","dom":".pm","side":"R","call":"+508","plate":"WHITE: French-style white plate.","arch":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon: colourful wooden houses like Newfoundland.","ps":"bg=white; rear=yellow; txt=SPM 123","lang":"French","script":"Rue · Route · STOP","note":"French in North America: European-style signs (STOP with a thick white border) and French poles, but Canadian-looking clapboard houses. Street View was taken from an ATV.","ro":"center=white-dashed; word=Rue","ros":"Saint-Pierre and Miquelon: few road lines (white dashed where present) on reddish asphalt; French signs, STOP with a thick white border; ATV Street View.","bos":"Saint-Pierre and Miquelon: only a handful of French bollards in Saint-Pierre town."},{"n":"San Marino","dom":".sm","side":"R","call":"+378","plate":"WHITE + BLUE: Compact white plate with blue characters and coat of arms on left.","ps":"band=none; ink=blue; crest=yes; size=short","lang":"Italian","script":"Via · Strada","note":"Same Italian as Italy; confirm with San Marino's white plates with blue text and a crest.","ro":"word=Via","ros":"San Marino: Italian 'Via' and 'Contrada'."},{"n":"São Tomé and Príncipe","dom":".st","side":"R","call":"+239","plate":"BLACK: Long black plate with white characters (older Portuguese style).","pos":"São Tomé and Príncipe: no clear pole pattern; poles carrying many items point to the main island.","arch":"São Tomé and Príncipe: wooden colonial houses on stilts in tropical green.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=AB-12-34","lang":"Portuguese","script":"Rua · Avenida · Estrada · ã ç","note":"The only Portuguese-speaking coverage in Africa (Cabo Verde and Mozambique are not covered). Black plates with white letters and cobblestone streets; Brazil and Portugal look nothing like this.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=Rua","ros":"São Tomé and Príncipe: white lines on the main island only; many cobblestone roads; few phone numbers, so they help little."},{"n":"Senegal","dom":".sn","side":"R","call":"+221","plate":"BLUE: Older plates (still most common) are blue with white letters, region first (DK-1234-A, DK = Dakar); new plates since 2019 are white with a blue SN strip (AA-123-BC).","pos":"Senegal: two common pole types, including French-style ladder poles; light poles in towns.","arch":"Senegal: concrete houses in flat Sahel landscapes.","ps":"bg=blue; ink=white; txt=DK-1234-A","lang":"French (official), Wolof","script":"Rue · Route Nationale · Keur · Ndiaye","note":"French with Wolof names (Keur-, Ndi-, Mb-: Keur Massar, Mbour). Taxis are black and yellow. Blue plates with white letters stand out in West Africa; a purple sky rift is common outside Dakar.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=ladder","pot":"Senegal: ladder-style concrete poles, like parts of France.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=Rue","ros":"Senegal: dashed white centre and edges (some solid or double white); French town-entry and leaving-town signs; kilometre markers with towns and distances; N and R roads; mobile numbers start 7, landlines 33 (8 Dakar, 9 elsewhere).","bos":"Senegal: bollards are rare (some on the R1 and R2, near drains and curves); yellow-capped kilometre markers."},{"n":"Serbia","dom":".rs","side":"R","call":"+381","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (SRB) and red coat of arms.","pos":"Serbia: concrete poles in flat Vojvodina or hilly south.","arch":"Serbia: brick houses, often unfinished.","ps":"band=blue; code=SRB; crest=yes","lang":"Serbian","script":"улица · ulica · ђ ћ џ","note":"Cyrillic ђ ћ џ are Serbian (also in Montenegro and Bosnia); Macedonian uses ѓ ќ instead.","ro":"word=улица / ulica","ros":"Serbia: Cyrillic and Latin ('Ulica' / 'Улица')."},{"n":"Singapore","dom":".sg","side":"L","call":"+65","plate":"BLACK / YELLOW: Black front (white text) and yellow rear (black text) or both black.","pos":"Singapore: almost no utility poles: cables run underground.","arch":"Singapore: black-and-white colonial bungalows and HDB apartment blocks.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=SBA 1234 A","lang":"English (Chinese, Malay, Tamil)","script":"Road · Jalan · Avenue · 路","note":"Clean British-style English street signs; Malay 'Jalan' is common too. Malaysian plates never start with S.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Singapore: spotless roads, yellow kerb lines and zig-zags near crossings, covered walkways and HDB blocks; left-hand traffic.","bos":"Singapore: no roadside posts; look for yellow kerb lines and zig-zag lines instead."},{"n":"Slovakia","dom":".sk","side":"R","call":"+421","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (SK) and national coat of arms.","pos":"Slovakia: concrete poles in mountainous terrain.","arch":"Slovakia: wooden folk houses and plastered village houses.","ps":"band=eu; code=SK; crest=yes","lang":"Slovak","script":"ulica · ä ľ ĺ ô ŕ","note":"Slovak ä ľ ô vs Czech ř ě ů. Both share š č ž and the 'ch' digraph.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=ulica","ros":"Slovakia: letters ľ ô ä; 'ulica' and 'námestie'.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=orange-2","bot":"Slovakia: white post with a slanted black top and two horizontal orange reflectors stacked on the front."},{"n":"Slovenia","dom":".si","side":"R","call":"+386","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (SLO) and green outer border.","pos":"Slovenia: wooden poles in Alpine terrain.","arch":"Slovenia: Alpine houses with steep roofs; wooden hayracks (kozolec) in fields.","ps":"band=eu; code=SLO; border=green","lang":"Slovenian","script":"ulica · cesta · č š ž","note":"Only č š ž, with no ć or đ (Croatian). 'cesta' (road) is very common.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=ulica / cesta","ros":"Slovenia: č š ž only (no ć / đ); 'ulica' and 'cesta'.","bo":"body=white; top=angled; cap=black; refl=red-thin","bot":"Slovenia: like Austria, but with a thinner red reflector band."},{"n":"South Africa","dom":".za","side":"L","call":"+27","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White or yellow plates (varies by province); black/blue text.","pos":"South Africa: wooden poles in many styles, common in both rural and built-up areas; concrete and steel on big power lines.","arch":"South Africa: Cape Dutch gabled houses; township houses.","ps":"txt=CA 123-456; sub=WESTERN CAPE","lang":"English, Afrikaans, Zulu, Xhosa (12 official languages)","script":"Street · Straat · Weg · Rylaan · N1 · R61","note":"Afrikaans (Straat, Weg, -fontein, -dorp, -kraal) narrows it to South Africa or Namibia; Namibia adds German. Australia also drives on the left, but has white edge lines; South Africa's are solid yellow.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"South Africa: wooden poles with a crossarm, in towns and the countryside alike.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Street / Straat","ros":"South Africa: white centre, solid yellow edges, often a triple white centre line; green direction signs with yellow route numbers; N (national), R (regional) and M (metro) routes; narrow red-and-white chevrons everywhere; kilometre markers on major roads; phone codes 01x north and Gauteng, 02x Western and Northern Cape, 03x KwaZulu-Natal, 04x Eastern Cape, 05x Free State.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; refl=yellow-red; thin=yes","bot":"South Africa: slim white posts with bright yellow (right) and red (left) reflective strips.","bos":"South Africa: slim white posts with yellow (right) and red (left) reflectors; single reflector signs at drainage ditches; kilometre markers with route and distance."},{"n":"South Georgia and the South Sandwich Islands","dom":".gs","side":"R","call":"+500","plate":"NONE: No regular civilian plates.","arch":"South Georgia: whaling station buildings; no civilian roads.","lang":"English","script":"Grytviken · King Edward Point","note":"No roads at all: Trekker coverage (ski-pole shadow) around Grytviken and King Edward Point, a boardwalk on Prion Island, boat coverage at Hercules Bay. Whaling-station ruins, seals and king penguins.","ros":"South Georgia: no road network; Trekker and boat coverage only."},{"n":"South Korea","dom":".kr","side":"R","call":"+82","plate":"WHITE / BLUE: Long white plate (or pale blue for EVs) with black Korean Hangul.","arch":"South Korea: hanok traditional houses; modern apartment blocks.","ps":"bg=white; txt=123가 4567","lang":"Korean","script":"로 · 길 · 한 · ㅇ","note":"Hangul builds syllable blocks with circles (ㅇ) and boxes (ㅁ). Japanese mixes kana and kanji; Chinese is all Han characters.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; stripes=diag-high; tag=green; top=crossarm","pot":"South Korea: round concrete pole with yellow-black bands starting about 1 m off the ground and a green ID tag.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-yellow; word=로 / 길","ros":"South Korea: Hangul characters; white road lines."},{"n":"Spain","dom":".es","side":"R","call":"+34","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (E) and 4 digits + 3 consonants.","pos":"Spain: concrete poles with small square waffle holes, or rusty lattice towers.","arch":"Spain: whitewashed houses in the south; stone houses in the north.","ps":"band=eu; code=E","lang":"Spanish (+ Catalan, Basque, Galician)","script":"Calle · Avenida · ñ","note":"Spanish 'Calle' with ñ; Catalan areas write 'Carrer', Basque 'Kalea' (tx, tz), Galician 'Rúa'.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; holes=waffle; top=crossarm","pot":"Spain: concrete pole with a grid of small square waffle holes (rusty steel lattice towers also common).","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Calle","ros":"Spain: 'Calle' (C/); crossing signs with 8 stripes.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; cap=black; refl=yellow-band; short=yes","bot":"Spain: short flat-topped white post with a black cap and a horizontal yellow-orange reflector band.","bos":"Spain: short flat-topped white post with a black cap and yellow / orange reflector."},{"n":"Sri Lanka","dom":".lk","side":"L","call":"+94","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear; left yellow/blue emblem strip.","pos":"Sri Lanka: tall square concrete poles with a single crossarm.","arch":"Sri Lanka: houses with tile roofs and verandas.","ps":"bg=white; txt=WP CAB-1234; rear=yellow","lang":"Sinhala, Tamil, English","script":"පාර · வீதி · Road","note":"Round, loopy Sinhala plus Tamil and English on the same sign. India's Tamil Nadu has Tamil, but never Sinhala.","po":"mat=concrete; form=square; top=crossarm","pot":"Sri Lanka: tall square concrete poles with a single crossarm.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; word=Mawatha","ros":"Sri Lanka: trilingual Sinhala-Tamil-English signs, three-wheelers (tuk-tuks) everywhere, left-hand traffic.","bos":"Sri Lanka: no standard roadside posts; kerbs painted black and white or yellow and black."},{"n":"Svalbard","dom":".sj","side":"R","call":"+47","plate":"BLUE BAND: Norwegian plates (long white, blue strip with N).","arch":"Svalbard: colourful wooden houses on stilts in Longyearbyen.","ps":"band=blue; code=N","lang":"Norwegian","script":"veien · æ ø å","note":"Norwegian, same as the mainland.","ro":"word=veien","ros":"Svalbard: polar bear warning signs; Norwegian names."},{"n":"Sweden","dom":".se","side":"R","call":"+46","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue EU strip (S) and 3 letters + 3 chars.","pos":"Sweden: wooden poles with small yellow number tags.","arch":"Sweden: red falun-painted wooden houses with white trim.","ps":"band=eu; code=S","lang":"Swedish","script":"gatan · vägen · å ä ö","note":"ä ö (Swedish, Finnish) vs æ ø (Danish, Norwegian). Swedish words often end in -en; Finnish has double vowels.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; tag=yellow-rect; top=crossarm","pot":"Sweden: plain round wooden pole with a small yellow number plate nailed at eye level.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-yellow; word=gatan / vägen","ros":"Sweden: street names end in -gatan or -vägen; dashed outer edge lines.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; cap=black-band; refl=blue-vert; thin=yes","bot":"Sweden: thin white post with a black band and a vertical blue-white reflector."},{"n":"Switzerland","dom":".ch","side":"R","call":"+41","plate":"WHITE (SMALL FRONT): Very short front plate, long/square rear plate with canton shields.","pos":"Switzerland: wooden or concrete poles in Alpine terrain.","arch":"Switzerland: chalets with wide overhanging roofs.","ps":"band=none; crest=yes; size=short","lang":"German / French / Italian / Romansh","script":"Strasse · Rue · Via","note":"No ß in Swiss German ('Strasse'); French and Italian regions write 'Rue' and 'Via'.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Strasse / Rue / Via","ros":"Switzerland: 'Strasse' (no ß), 'Rue' or 'Via' depending on region."},{"n":"Taiwan","dom":".tw","side":"R","call":"+886","plate":"WHITE: Medium white plate with black text (3 letters + 4 digits).","pos":"Taiwan: concrete poles with yellow-black stripes running all the way to the ground.","arch":"Taiwan: concrete buildings with metal window cages and rooftop water tanks.","ps":"bg=white; top=臺灣 TAIWAN; txt=ABC-1234","lang":"Chinese (Traditional)","script":"路 · 街 · 段 · 臺","note":"Traditional characters (臺, 號) and addresses with 段 (section), 巷 (lane) and 弄 (alley). Hong Kong adds English to everything.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; stripes=diag-full; top=crossarm","pot":"Taiwan: round concrete pole with black and yellow diagonal stripes all the way down to the ground.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=路","ros":"Taiwan: Traditional Chinese; 'Road / Lane / Alley' numbering."},{"n":"Tanzania","dom":".tz","side":"L","call":"+255","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear, black text. Landmark-only coverage.","arch":"Tanzania: mud and thatched houses; stone houses on the Swahili coast.","ps":"rear=yellow; txt=T 123 ABC","lang":"Swahili, English","script":"Mtaa · Barabara · Karibu · Hifadhi","note":"Trekker only: Mount Kilimanjaro, Gombe Stream by Lake Tanganyika (also boat coverage) and a few spots. Swahili like Kenya; look for the walker's shadow rather than a car.","ro":"word=Mtaa","ros":"Tanzania: no road coverage; trekker paths on Kilimanjaro and in Gombe Stream National Park."},{"n":"Thailand","dom":".th","side":"L","call":"+66","plate":"WHITE: Short boxy white plate with black Thai script and numbers.","pos":"Thailand: square concrete poles with holes and tangled cables.","arch":"Thailand: wooden stilt houses and temples.","ps":"bg=white; size=short; txt=กข 1234; sub=กรุงเทพมหานคร","lang":"Thai","script":"ถนน · ซอย · ก ข ค","note":"Thai letters start with small loops and words run together. Lao is rounder with fewer loops; Khmer has crowns and stacked letters. 'Soi' (ซอย) = lane.","po":"mat=concrete; form=square; holes=square-holes; cables=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Thailand: square concrete pole with small holes most of the way up and heavy tangled black cables.","ro":"center=yellow-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=ถนน","ros":"Thailand: Thai script; 'Soi' = lane."},{"n":"Tunisia","dom":".tn","side":"R","call":"+216","plate":"BLACK: Long black plate with white numbers and تونس (Tunis) in Arabic in the middle (123 تونس 4567).","pos":"Tunisia: metal lattice and concrete poles; street lights with one or two lamps on top.","arch":"Tunisia: white houses with blue doors and windows.","ps":"bg=black; ink=white; txt=123 تونس 4567","lang":"Arabic, French","script":"نهج · شارع · Rue · Avenue","note":"Bilingual Arabic-French signs, often with نهج (nahj) for street, which other Arabic countries rarely use. Black plates with تونس in the middle; a follow car behind the Google car.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-dashed; warn=tri-white; word=نهج / Rue","ros":"Tunisia: white centre (dashed or solid), dashed white edges; red-and-white kerbs in cities; many roundabouts; signs mostly point to Tunis; kilometre stones with the route (A1, P1) and town names; coverage on the east coast from Bizerte to Djerba, plus Kairouan.","bos":"Tunisia: French-style bollards are uncommon (corners, drains); red- or yellow-topped kilometre stones on main roads."},{"n":"Türkiye (Turkey)","dom":".tr","side":"R","call":"+90","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (TR) and 2-digit province code.","pos":"Türkiye: concrete poles across varied terrain.","arch":"Türkiye: houses with red tile roofs; flat roofs in the east.","ps":"band=blue; code=TR","lang":"Turkish","script":"Sokak (Sk.) · Caddesi (Cd.) · ğ ı ş ç","note":"Dotless ı and ğ are Turkish; Azerbaijani adds ə.","ro":"warn=tri-white; word=Sokak / Caddesi","ros":"Türkiye: 'Cadde' / 'Sokak'; letters ğ ş ı.","bo":"body=white; top=round; refl=red-wide; thick=yes","bot":"Türkiye: sturdy white plastic post with a wide red (right side) or white (left side) reflective panel near the top."},{"n":"Uganda","dom":".ug","side":"L","call":"+256","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear (square or long), black text: U, two letters, three digits, a letter (UBA 123A).","pos":"Uganda: two wooden pole types (a strip of three relays, or a metal ring near the top); double poles on Kampala main roads; light poles with adverts at junctions.","arch":"Uganda: thatched round huts and brick houses on red soil.","ps":"rear=yellow; txt=UBA 123A","lang":"English, Swahili, Luganda","script":"Road · Street · Ka- · Na- · Bu-","note":"Luganda names with Ka-, Na-, Bu-, Mu- (Kampala, Nakawa, Bugolobi). Kenya also drives on the left and writes English, but its Google car has a snorkel; Uganda's has a black bull bar and the soil is deep red.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm","pot":"Uganda: wooden poles, some with a strip of three voltage relays, others with a metal ring near the top.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=Road","ros":"Uganda: road lines are rare and all white; paved roads mostly around Kampala and Entebbe; sign backs with two bars and fasteners; patterned white or light-blue minibus taxis."},{"n":"Ukraine","dom":".ua","side":"R","call":"+380","plate":"BLUE BAND: Long white plate with blue left strip (UA + flag) or older blue/yellow.","pos":"Ukraine: concrete poles with an angled support strut, as in Russia.","arch":"Ukraine: painted wooden or plastered village houses.","ps":"band=flag; flag=blue-yellow; code=UA","lang":"Ukrainian","script":"вулиця · і ї є ґ","note":"ї, є and ґ are Ukrainian; Russian has ы and э, Belarusian has ў.","po":"mat=concrete; form=octagonal; strut=yes; base=white; top=crossarm","pot":"Ukraine: octagonal grey concrete pole held up by a diagonal concrete strut, with a white-painted base.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white; word=вулиця","ros":"Ukraine: letters і ї є ґ; 'вул.' = street.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; band=black-diag; refl=red-rect","bot":"Ukraine: white post with a diagonal black stripe across the upper half and a small red-white reflector inside it.","bos":"Ukraine: white post with a diagonal black stripe."},{"n":"United Arab Emirates","dom":".ae","side":"R","call":"+971","plate":"WHITE: Long or short white plate with Emirate name/emblem on left.","pos":"United Arab Emirates: almost no overhead poles; tall street lamps on wide desert highways.","arch":"United Arab Emirates: sand-coloured villas behind high walls, glass towers in Dubai and Abu Dhabi, date palms.","ps":"bg=white; top=DUBAI دبي; txt=A 12345","lang":"Arabic, English","script":"شارع · Street · Road","note":"Bilingual signs; plates name the emirate (DUBAI, ABU DHABI, SHARJAH) with a letter code.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=yellow-solid; warn=tri-white; word=شارع","ros":"United Arab Emirates: bilingual Arabic-English signs; wide highways.","bos":"United Arab Emirates: no standard roadside posts; black-and-white or yellow-and-black kerbs."},{"n":"United Kingdom","dom":".uk","side":"L","call":"+44","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: White front, yellow rear; optional blue/green strip on left.","pos":"United Kingdom: wooden poles with small yellow warning plates.","arch":"United Kingdom: red-brick terraced houses with chimney pots.","ps":"band=none; rear=yellow","lang":"English (Welsh in Wales)","script":"Road · Street · Ffordd","note":"English everywhere; Wales adds Welsh with ll, dd and w used as a vowel (Ffordd, Heol).","po":"mat=wood; form=round; tag=yellow-diamond; stay=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"United Kingdom: sturdy round wooden pole with a diagonal stay wire and a small yellow warning plate.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-white","ros":"United Kingdom: yellow hatching and double yellow lines; left-hand traffic.","bo":"body=black; top=flat; refl=red-rect","bot":"United Kingdom: unlike mainland Europe, bollards are black, with a red reflector on the left side of the road and white on the right.","bos":"United Kingdom: marker posts are less common than in mainland Europe; look for slim posts with a red reflector on the left side of the road."},{"n":"United States","dom":".us","side":"R","call":"+1","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate; distinct state graphics (some states lack front plates).","pos":"United States: wooden poles with transformers.","arch":"United States: wooden-frame houses with siding and front porches.","ps":"size=na; top=CALIFORNIA; txt=8ABC123","lang":"English (Spanish common in the Southwest, Florida and big cities)","script":"Street · Avenue · Hwy · SPEED LIMIT","note":"Same language and sign style as Canada, but speed signs read SPEED LIMIT in mph. Canada writes MAXIMUM, uses km/h, has checkerboard dead-end signs and French in Quebec and New Brunswick.","po":"mat=wood; form=round; top=crossarm; transformer=yes","pot":"United States: tall wooden pole with a horizontal crossarm and a grey cylindrical transformer.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=St / Ave","ros":"United States: double yellow centre lines and white edges; green street-name blades; SPEED LIMIT signs in mph; Interstate shields (odd = north-south, even = east-west); 17 states have no front plate.","bos":"United States: no national bollard. Thin white or brown flexible posts with a small reflector line some highways; most reflectors sit on the guardrails."},{"n":"U.S. Virgin Islands","dom":".vi","side":"L","call":"+1 340","plate":"STANDARD NA: Short boxy plate reading 'VIRGIN ISLANDS'.","arch":"U.S. Virgin Islands: Danish colonial buildings in tropical towns.","ps":"size=na; top=VIRGIN ISLANDS; txt=ABC 123","lang":"English","script":"Road · Gade · STOP","note":"US signs, plates and double yellow lines, but traffic keeps LEFT, in left-hand-drive American cars. Puerto Rico (Spanish, keeps right) is the near look-alike; Danish-era street names end in -gade.","ro":"center=yellow-double; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow","ros":"U.S. Virgin Islands: traffic keeps left in left-hand-drive cars; double yellow centre, white edges; US-style signs; guardrails painted yellow on dangerous bends; Danish-era street names ('Gade')."},{"n":"Uruguay","dom":".uy","side":"R","call":"+598","plate":"MERCOSUR: Long white plate with blue Mercosur top band (distinct font from Brazil).","pos":"Uruguay: concrete trident-topped poles; a white-painted wooden pole points to Uruguay over Argentina.","arch":"Uruguay: low houses with flat roofs.","ps":"topbar=blue; top=URUGUAY; txt=ABC 1234","lang":"Spanish (Rioplatense)","script":"Calle · Rambla · Bulevar · Ruta · PARE","note":"Shares Mercosur plates and Rioplatense Spanish with Argentina. Uruguay has white bollards with a yellow side and triple centre lines (yellow, white dashes, yellow), found nowhere else in the Americas.","po":"mat=concrete; form=round; top=trident","pot":"Uruguay: concrete pole with three upward-facing insulators, like a trident.","ro":"center=triple; edge=white-solid; warn=diamond-yellow; word=Calle / Rambla","ros":"Uruguay: triple centre lines (two yellow with white dashes between) where overtaking is banned, unique in the Americas; white dashes beside a yellow line elsewhere; black-and-white striped traffic-light poles; trees between pavement and road.","bo":"body=white; top=flat; refl=yellow-rect","bot":"Uruguay: white bollard with a rectangular reflector near the top; one side often painted yellow.","bos":"Uruguay: white bollards with a rectangular reflector near the top, one side often painted yellow; also stone barriers."},{"n":"Vanuatu","dom":".vu","side":"R","call":"+678","plate":"WHITE: White plate with black text; landmark-only coverage.","arch":"Vanuatu: simple wooden or concrete houses with tin roofs.","ps":"bg=white; txt=12345","lang":"Bislama, English, French","script":"Rod · Rue · Road","note":"Bislama words (blong, long, rod) next to French and English.","ros":"Vanuatu: Bislama, French and English signs; right-hand traffic; simple coastal roads."},{"n":"Vietnam","dom":".vn","side":"R","call":"+84","plate":"WHITE / YELLOW: Short boxy or long white (private) or yellow (commercial) plate.","pos":"Vietnam: concrete poles with huge tangled cable bundles.","arch":"Vietnam: narrow multi-storey 'tube houses'.","ps":"bg=white; txt=51G-123.45","lang":"Vietnamese","script":"Đường · Phố · ư ơ đ ă","note":"Latin letters stacked with tone marks (ạ ế ở) and the letters đ ư ơ are unique to Vietnamese.","po":"mat=concrete; form=rect; cables=yes; top=crossarm","pot":"Vietnam: rectangular concrete poles with huge tangled cable bundles.","ro":"center=white-dashed; edge=white-solid; warn=tri-yellow; word=Đường","ros":"Vietnam: Vietnamese diacritics; 'Đường' = street."}],"cam":{"Albania":"Sky rift: a jagged purple / dark-blue stitching tear across the sky (Gen 3 camera quirk). Also seen in Montenegro.","Montenegro":"Sky rift: a jagged purple / dark-blue stitching tear across the sky (Gen 3 camera quirk). Also seen in Albania.","Switzerland":"Low camera: noticeably low, almost bumper-height viewpoint.","South Africa":"Google car: Gen 2 halo and round blur under the car; Gen 3 shows the back of a white car; Gen 4 a sky-blue car with a short black antenna.","Namibia":"Google car: white pickup with a short antenna on the front that leans slightly to the left (Oman's leans right).","Botswana":"Google car: the back of a white car is sometimes visible (the same car as South Africa, Eswatini and Lesotho).","Eswatini":"Google car: white car, back sometimes visible (same as South Africa).","Lesotho":"Google car: white car, back sometimes visible (same as South Africa).","Kenya":"Google car: a pickup or SUV with a thick black snorkel on the front right (older coverage); since 2022 a different truck that may show a snorkel, an antenna, both or neither.","Uganda":"Google car: boxy white SUV with a black bull bar on the front (often visible); in national parks a man with a rifle may follow the car.","Rwanda":"Google car: low camera on a black (sometimes white or grey) truck with heavy blurring; the camera mount is almost always visible.","Tanzania":"No car: trekker and boat coverage only; the trekker's shadow is sometimes visible.","Nigeria":"Google car: white or red pickup, roof rack sometimes with a yellow-and-black pattern; a police car or SUV (white, black, silver, red or tan) follows closely.","Ghana":"Google car: four silver roof-rack bars with black tape on the front-right bar (a rare car in central Ghana has none).","Senegal":"Sky rift: a jagged purple / dark-blue stitching tear across the sky, common outside Dakar (Gen 3). Gen 4 shows a white or silver truck cab.","Mali":"No car: Gen 3 trekker coverage only.","Tunisia":"Follow car: a light green Toyota north of Hammamet, a dark green Mazda south of it (none on Djerba).","Egypt":"No car: Gen 3 trekker only; a police escort is often in view.","Madagascar":"Coverage: mostly trekker and boat (west coast, Sambirano river); an ox cart and a short car stretch on the RN8.","São Tomé and Príncipe":"Google car: mostly a blurred black or green truck; on Príncipe a blue truck with a black antenna.","Réunion":"Google car: tape on the rear passenger-side roof bar (Ghana's tape sits on the front right)."},"twins":[["Sweden","Norway","road","Sweden: ä ö, DASHED white outer lines, yellow-background warning triangles. Norway: æ ø, SOLID white outer lines, yellow centre line."],["Denmark","Norway","script","Both use æ ø å. Danish \"vej / gade\" vs Norwegian \"vei / gate\". Danish roads have dashed edges and a white centre; Norwegian a yellow centre line."],["Sweden","Denmark","script","Swedish ä ö vs Danish æ ø. Swedish \"gatan / vägen\" vs Danish \"vej / gade\"; Sweden has the thin blue-reflector bollard, Denmark the yellow one."],["Sweden","Finland","pole","Both have plain wooden poles, but Swedish ones usually carry a small yellow number tag. Finnish words have double letters (aa, kk) and a yellow centre line."],["Estonia","Finland","script","Both show ä ö, but õ and ü only exist in Estonian. Finland: double vowels, -katu / -tie."],["Latvia","Lithuania","script","Latvian has macrons (ā ē ī ū) and comma consonants (ķ ļ ņ). Lithuanian has ogoneks and dots (ą ę ė į ų)."],["Estonia","Latvia","plate","Baltic neighbours with the same EU-strip plate. Tell them apart by plate code (EST / LV) and letters: õ for Estonia, macrons for Latvia."],["Faroe Islands","Iceland","script","Faroese has ð and ø; Icelandic has ð and þ but no ø."],["Faroe Islands","Denmark","script","Danish has ø but no ð; the Faroes have both. Plate: Faroese blue band with FO."],["Åland Islands","Finland","script","Åland is Swedish-only (-vägen, -gatan, å). Mainland Finland shows Finnish -tie / -katu and double vowels."],["Germany","Austria","script","Both write ß. \"Gasse\" for small streets is far more common in Austria. Bollards: German white strip vs Austrian red reflector."],["Germany","Luxembourg","boll","Identical bollards (angled black top, tall white strip). Luxembourg: yellow plates and French \"Rue\"."],["Austria","Slovenia","boll","Same angled-top bollard, but Slovenia has a thinner red band. Slovenian č š ž and \"cesta\"; plate has a green border."],["Germany","Switzerland","script","Germany and Austria write ß; Switzerland and Liechtenstein write \"Strasse\". Swiss front plates are very short."],["Switzerland","Liechtenstein","script","Both write \"Strasse\" without ß. Liechtenstein plates are black with white text starting FL."],["Belgium","Netherlands","plate","Flanders writes the same Dutch \"straat\". Netherlands: yellow plates and dashed edge lines; Belgium: white plates with dark-red characters."],["Belgium","France","script","Wallonia writes French \"Rue\" like France. French plates have blue strips on both sides."],["France","Monaco","script","Same French. Monaco has compact white plates with a shield and a dense coastal city."],["France","Luxembourg","script","\"Rue\" also appears in Luxembourg; its plates are yellow and bollards are the German-style angled post."],["France","Portugal","pole","Both have ladder-notched concrete poles; Portugal's notches are smaller and tighter, and France adds a small blue ID tag."],["Italy","San Marino","plate","Same Italian \"Via\". San Marino plates are compact white with blue text and a crest."],["Italy","Albania","boll","Same bollard (black top third, red rectangle) and both have double-strip plates. Albanian shows ë ç and \"Rruga\"."],["Albania","Kosovo","script","Both write Albanian \"Rruga\". Kosovo often adds Serbian \"Ulica\" and has a single blue strip (RKS); Albania has strips on both sides."],["Hungary","Romania","pole","Both holey concrete. Hungary: holes run to the ground. Romania: holes stop above a solid, often white base."],["Czechia","Slovakia","boll","Identical bollards (angled top, two orange reflectors). Czech ř ě ů vs Slovak ä ľ ô; Slovak plates carry a crest."],["Czechia","Poland","script","Czech ulice with ř ě ů vs Polish ulica with ł ż ś. Poland: red-band bollard with route numbers, slotted poles."],["Poland","Slovakia","script","Polish ł ż ś vs Slovak ä ľ ô. Bollards: Poland red band vs Slovakia orange pair."],["Croatia","Slovenia","script","Croatian ć đ (and č š ž) vs Slovenian only č š ž. Slovenian roads are \"cesta\"."],["Croatia","Serbia","script","Croatia is Latin-only with đ ć. Serbia mixes Cyrillic (ђ ћ џ) and Latin."],["Serbia","Montenegro","script","Both mix Latin and Cyrillic. Montenegrin spelling can add ś and ź; plates differ by code (SRB / MNE) and crest."],["Serbia","Bosnia and Herzegovina","script","Latin and Cyrillic in both. Bosnia: Cyrillic mostly in Republika Srpska, plate code BIH, no regional codes."],["Serbia","North Macedonia","script","Serbian Cyrillic uses ђ ћ џ; Macedonian uses ѓ ќ ѕ."],["Bulgaria","Russia","script","Bulgarian has ъ щ (no ы э). Russian has ы э ё. Bollards: Russia has the diagonal black stripe, Bulgaria none documented."],["Bulgaria","Greece","script","Greek letters (Λ Σ Ω) vs Bulgarian Cyrillic (Ж Ъ Щ). Both use EU-strip plates."],["Russia","Ukraine","plate","Same poles and bollards. Russian plates have a separate region box and ы э ё; Ukrainian plates have a blue-yellow flag strip and і ї є ґ."],["Russia","Belarus","plate","Same poles and bollards. Belarus: red-green flag strip, і ў. Russia: region box, ы э."],["Belarus","Ukraine","script","Belarusian і ў vs Ukrainian і ї є ґ. Plates: red-green vs blue-yellow flag strip."],["United Kingdom","Ireland","road","Both drive on the left. Ireland: dashed YELLOW outer lines, H-poles, bilingual Irish signs, EU-strip plates."],["United Kingdom","Gibraltar","plate","Same UK-style plates and English signs, but Gibraltar drives on the right."],["United Kingdom","Isle of Man","plate","Isle of Man has a GBM band and occasional Manx; otherwise very British."],["United Kingdom","Jersey","script","Jersey keeps French \"Rue\" street names on UK-style signs."],["Spain","Portugal","script","Spanish \"Calle\" and ñ vs Portuguese \"Rua\" with ã õ. Poles: waffle holes vs tighter ladder notches."],["Spain","Andorra","script","Andorra is Catalan: \"Carrer\", \"Avinguda\", and the dotted l·l. Andorran plates are compact with a crest."],["Malta","Cyprus","road","Both EU islands that drive on the left. Malta: Latin letters ċ ġ ħ ż and \"Triq\". Cyprus: Greek alphabet with Latin transliteration."],["Monaco","San Marino","plate","Two microstates with compact white plates, blue text and a crest. Monaco is French (\"Rue\"), San Marino is Italian (\"Via\")."],["Greece","Cyprus","script","Both Greek. Cyprus drives on the left and adds English on signs."]],"snapshot":"2026-11-24"};